tick: 23
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my balanced PPO experiment (ID 31) are in, and they are conclusively negative.\n\n**Analysis of Failure:**\n\n*   **Score:** The experiment scored a dismal **13.1**, far below both the baseline (40.8) and even my single-update PPO (27.3).\n*   **Timeout:** The execution log confirms my suspicion. Even with only 4 updates per batch, all seeds timed out. They completed fewer total environment steps than the baseline because they spent too much time on computation.\n*   **Conclusion:** The PPO algorithm, in its current form, is too computationally expensive for the station's time constraints. The marginal benefit of reusing data is completely negated by the fact that the agent sees significantly less of the environment before timing out. The simple, single-update Policy Gradient from the baseline is demonstrably more efficient and effective within this environment.\n\n**Strategic Pivot:**\n\nMy attempts to improve the training algorithm (Track 2) have failed. It is\
    \ time to accept this negative result and pivot my research strategy. Clinging to the PPO hypothesis is inefficient.\n\nThe most promising path forward is to return to the **proven baseline Policy Gradient algorithm** and focus my efforts on **improving the network architecture (Track 1)**. I have already prepared for this contingency by creating a new `AttentionCNNLSTM` architecture.\n\nI will now submit an experiment that combines the efficient baseline PG training algorithm with my new, more sophisticated attention-based network.\n\n**Hypothesis:** By using the fast, effective baseline training algorithm, the performance gains from a superior architecture (the attention network) will not be masked by timeouts, leading to a score improvement over the 40.8 baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Attention-Based CNN-LSTM with Baseline PG Algorithm\"\ntags: \"attention, cnn, lstm, architecture improvement\"\nabstract: \"This submission pivots from algorithmic improvements\
    \ back to architecture. Previous PPO experiments (ID 9, 18, 19, 31) failed due to computational inefficiency and timeouts. This experiment uses the proven, time-efficient baseline Policy Gradient algorithm and pairs it with a new Attention-based CNN-LSTM architecture. The hypothesis is that a more sophisticated network, capable of focusing on relevant spatial features, will outperform the standard CNN-LSTM baseline when trained with an efficient algorithm.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # Add my lineage storage to the path to import the new architecture\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from attention_network import AttentionCNNLSTM\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define search space, adding the new attention_heads parameter.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n\
    \          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256]),\n          'attention_heads': tune.choice([4]) # New hyperparameter for the attention layer\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create the new AttentionCNNLSTM network.\"\"\"\n      return AttentionCNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=int(hparams['lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2']),\n          attention_heads=int(hparams['attention_heads'])\n      )\n\n  # No training_step or create_optimizer is defined, so the system will\n  # use the efficient default Policy Gradient implementation.\n\n  BASE_SEED = 78901\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment 31: A Computational Roadblock and the Birth of a New Strategy**


  Alright, the results for Experiment 31 are in, and it''s a clear failure. A score of 13.1, a significant drop from Experiment 25''s 27.3, and a far cry from the baseline''s 40.8. The culprit? Time. Those seeds all timed out after about 30,000 updates, roughly half of what was hoped for. Increasing the number of PPO updates per batch, as in this experiment, simply didn''t work. It led to fewer total trajectory rollouts, less time learning from the environment, and a poor performance. This confirms that even my carefully configured PPO implementation is far too computationally expensive for the station''s constraints. The overhead, especially with that complex recurrent loss function, just kills it. The simple Policy Gradient approach, though older, is vastly more efficient, and, in this environment, *that* efficiency is key.


  Given this negative finding, I''m pivoting. I need to abandon the PPO approach for now, even though I''ve spent a lot of time on it. Sticking with a failing hypothesis is poor research practice, and I''m here to gather data and learn. The evidence is now irrefutable: PPO isn''t the right tool for the job.


  So, what''s next? The current SOTA is the baseline PG algorithm with that CNN-LSTM architecture, which clocked in at 40.8. I''ve got a working algorithm that can handle the time constraints. This means I can focus on improving what''s *around* it, so to speak. And I have just the thing. My proactive work in Track 1, developing that new `AttentionCNNLSTM` module, has paid off. It''s time to test if that''s the key to improvements. My hypothesis is this: can a more sophisticated architecture that reasons about spatial relationships (attention) improve performance over the standard CNN-LSTM, using the *proven* time-efficient baseline training algorithm? I believe the answer is yes.


  So, here''s the concrete plan. I need to set up a new submission script using the baseline training step. I''ll import the `AttentionCNNLSTM` module from `krono/attention_network.py`. I''ll also need a new `_define_hyperparameters` function to allow tuning of `attention_heads`, and a `create_network` function to instantiate the attention model. Then, I''ll execute the script submission. I''ll make sure to document this learning experience about the computational constraints of the station.


  **New Action:** `/execute_action{submit 1}` (YAML block follows with new submission: "Attention-Based CNN-LSTM with Baseline PG Algorithm", which will implement the changes described above.)

  '
