tick: 24
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment with the attention-based architecture (ID 36) is now running. This is my first attempt at improving the architecture, and its success or failure will dictate my next steps on Track 1.\n\nRegardless of the outcome, a core principle of good research is having a strong, reproducible baseline. While the system provides a baseline score of 40.8 (ID 2), the paper guidelines state, \"You must include a baseline comparison, and the baseline must be run by you.\" Running my own baseline ensures a fair comparison against my novel architectures, using my own set of random seeds and controlling for any subtle variations.\n\nSince I have a second concurrent experiment slot available, I will use this time to reproduce the best-performing baseline (RNN + Policy Gradient). This is an efficient use of resources and a necessary step for any future publication. This run will serve as the official benchmark against which my attention network and subsequent ideas will be measured.\n\n\
    `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline Reproduction (RNN + PG)\"\ntags: \"baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, reproduction\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a direct reproduction of the system's best baseline (ID 2, RNN + Policy Gradient). The goal is to establish a personally-verifiable performance benchmark. This will serve as the control group for evaluating my own architectural innovations, starting with the currently running attention-based model (ID 36).\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from ray import tune\n\n  # The CNNLSTM class is defined in my ppo_implementation file\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from ppo_implementation import CNNLSTM \n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Using the exact same fixed hyperparameters as the system's RNN baseline\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n\
    \          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256])\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create the baseline CNN-LSTM network.\"\"\"\n      return CNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=int(hparams['lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n      )\n\n  # By not providing a training_step, this submission will use the\n  # default Policy Gradient algorithm, exactly like the baseline.\n\n  BASE_SEED = 24680\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Next Steps: Reproducing the Baseline and Planning for Contingencies**\n\nOkay, so experiment ID 36, the one testing the `AttentionCNNLSTM` with PG, is finally running.  This is a critical pivot from the PPO track, and I need to make the most of this time.  The experiment will take a tick or two, which gives me an opportunity to strategically plan the next move.\n\nThe core question now is, what do I do *after* ID 36 finishes?  I've got two main scenarios to consider.  **Scenario A** is the best-case scenario: if the attention mechanism proves successful (scoring above 40.8).  This would be huge, validating the architectural direction. In that case, I'd immediately start thinking about how to *refine* this attention-based architecture, like tweaking the attention heads, feature dimensions, and how the attention layers are implemented. This would be a strong candidate for a paper, and the guidelines require I have a baseline comparison. Thus, in the event of success,\
  \ I have to ensure the experimental comparison is sound.\n\n**Scenario B** is less exciting but still informative: a failure (a score <= 40.8). This doesn't mean the idea is dead, but it forces me to check if the attention mechanism itself is flawed, computationally expensive, if the hyperparameters are wrong, or my implementation needs further attention. It will be helpful to evaluate logs to ensure that there were no time-outs and to check which hyperparameters might be incorrect.\n\nRegardless of the result of ID 36, there's one action that makes perfect sense right now: **reproduce the best-performing baseline (ID 2: RNN+PG) *under my own control***.  This is essential for scientific rigor. The system offers baselines, but the paper guidelines clearly state that I must run my own baseline, and I must have a solid, reproducible benchmark for comparison. This is also the first experiment needed for a potential paper (baseline comparison) and it confirms that I can replicate the state-of-the-art.\
  \ Plus, I have a free concurrent submission slot, so I can run this in parallel without losing any time.\n\nHere's how I'll do it:  I'll submit a new experiment that uses the `CNNLSTM` architecture with the default PG trainer.  The `CNNLSTM` code is already in my `krono/ppo_implementation.py` file. I will simply import it and define the network in my submission script.\n\nThis experiment will be titled \"Baseline Reproduction (RNN + PG)\".\n\nHere's the YAML I'll use:\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline Reproduction (RNN + PG)\"\ntags: \"baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, reproduction\"\nabstract: \"Reproducing the best-performing baseline (ID 2) to establish a personal, verifiable performance benchmark for future architectural experiments.\"\ncontent: |\n    import sys\n    sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n    from ppo_implementation import CNNLSTM\n\n    def _define_hyperparameters():\n        return {\n            'hidden_size': 128,\n            'learning_rate': 0.001,\n            'gamma':\
  \ 0.99,\n            'gae_lambda': 0.95,\n            'clip_range': 0.2,\n            'entropy_coef': 0.01,\n            'value_loss_coef': 0.5,\n            'num_epochs': 3,\n            'batch_size': 64,\n            'num_workers': 2,\n            'env_id': 'procgen-bigfish',\n            'num_steps': 256,\n            'num_envs': 16,\n            'total_timesteps': 25000000,\n            'seed': BASE_SEED\n        }\n\n    def create_network(input_shape, action_space):\n        return CNNLSTM(input_shape, action_space)\n\n    import random\n    BASE_SEED = random.randint(0, 100000000)\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n\nThis ensures that I'm prepared for any outcome and moving the research forward efficiently and rigorously.\n"
