tick: 25
role: model
parts:
- text: "The result for my attention network experiment (ID 36) is in, and it is another significant failure.\n\n**Analysis of Failure (ID 36):**\n\n*   **Score:** The score of **11.0** is catastrophic. It's worse than all my previous attempts and even the pure CNN baseline (25.0).\n*   **Performance:** The training logs show the solve rate never improved, indicating the agent failed to learn meaningful policies. The training time (~960s) is slightly longer than the RNN baseline (~800s), suggesting the attention mechanism adds computational overhead, but it did not time out. The failure is therefore architectural, not computational.\n*   **Hypothesis:** My implementation of self-attention was likely too naive. Applying attention to the entire `8x8` grid creates a sequence of 64 tokens. This might introduce too much noise and complexity for the agent to learn useful relationships, especially early in training. The residual connections and layer normalization I included were good practice,\
    \ but not enough to overcome a flawed application of the concept.\n\n**Current Situation:**\nMy baseline reproduction (ID 37) is still running. I have now conducted five experiments, all of which have failed to improve upon the system baseline. Four of these were attempts to implement a better algorithm (PPO) and one was an attempt at a better architecture (Attention). Both research tracks have so far yielded negative results.\n\nIt is time for a deep reflection on my methodology. I have been quick to propose complex solutions without fully understanding the subtleties of the problem. My next step is not to propose another complex model, but to take a step back and conduct a simpler, more targeted experiment to truly understand *why* the baseline works.\n\n**New Plan: Ablation Study**\n\nI will perform an ablation study on the successful `CNNLSTM` baseline. The most complex part of that network is the deep stack of residual CNN blocks. What if this complexity is unnecessary?\n\n**Hypothesis:**\
    \ A simpler CNN backbone might perform just as well as the complex residual one, suggesting that the LSTM is doing most of the heavy lifting. If this is true, future architectural improvements can be built upon a simpler, faster foundation.\n\nI will create a version of the `CNNLSTM` with a very simple CNN: just two convolutional layers, no residual connections. I will use the baseline PG algorithm. This will test the importance of the CNN's depth.\n\nFirst, I'll write the simple network to a new file in my storage, then submit the experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/simple_network.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class SimpleCNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      An ablation study network. Uses a very simple CNN backbone to test the\n      importance of the complex residual blocks in the baseline.\n      \"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1:\
    \ int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # Initialize RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)), 'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features))}\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask), 'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask)}\n\n          # 1. Simplified CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten for MLP\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n  \
    \        \n          # 2. LSTM and Output Heads (same as baseline)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(features=self.lstm_features)\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation Study: Simple CNN + LSTM\"\ntags: \"ablation, cnn, lstm, architecture\"\nabstract: \"After a series of failed experiments with complex algorithms and architectures, this submission takes a step back. It is an ablation study on the successful RNN baseline (ID 2). This experiment replaces the deep residual CNN backbone with a simple two-layer CNN to test the\
    \ hypothesis that the LSTM component is the primary driver of performance. The goal is to understand the baseline's core strengths before attempting further innovation.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from ray import tune\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from simple_network import SimpleCNNLSTM \n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256])\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create the simplified CNN-LSTM network for the ablation study.\"\"\"\n      return SimpleCNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=int(hparams['lstm_features']),\n          cnn_features_1=int(hparams['cnn_features_1']),\n          cnn_features_2=int(hparams['cnn_features_2'])\n      )\n\n  # Uses the default\
    \ Policy Gradient algorithm.\n\n  BASE_SEED = 35791\n```"
