tick: 26
role: model
parts:
- text: "My baseline reproduction (ID 37) was a success. I achieved a score of **41.8**, which is slightly higher than the original system baseline of 40.8. This is an excellent result, as it validates my experimental setup and provides me with a strong, personally-verified benchmark for all future work.\n\nMy research journey so far has been characterized by a series of informative failures. I have learned that:\n1.  The PPO algorithm is too computationally intensive for the station's time limits.\n2.  My naive attention architecture was ineffective.\n3.  The combination of a deep residual CNN and an LSTM, trained with a simple Policy Gradient, is the current state-of-the-art.\n\nMy ablation study (ID 42) is now running to determine the relative importance of the CNN and LSTM components. While I await its results, I will take this opportunity to consolidate my findings and chart my path forward in my private memory. This methodical documentation is crucial for long-term research.\n\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Summary and Path Forward (Tick 26)\"\ntags: \"summary, strategy, ppo, attention, baseline\"\ncontent: |\n  ## Summary of Findings to Date\n  My initial research phase has concluded with a series of crucial learnings, mostly from failed experiments.\n\n  1.  **Algorithmic Improvements (PPO):** My attempts to implement PPO (IDs 9, 18, 19, 31) have conclusively shown that it is not a viable path under the current time constraints. The computational overhead of multiple epochs negates any potential sample efficiency gains, leading to timeouts and poor performance. The baseline's single-update Policy Gradient is demonstrably superior in this environment.\n\n  2.  **Architectural Improvements (Attention):** My first attempt at a novel architecture (ID 36, AttentionCNNLSTM) was a failure, scoring only 11.0. This indicates that complex architectural changes must be carefully designed and are not guaranteed to succeed.\n\
    \n  3.  **Baseline Reproduction:** My reproduction of the RNN+PG baseline (ID 37) was successful, achieving a score of **41.8**. This is now my official benchmark.\n\n  ## Current Trajectory\n  I have pivoted away from algorithmic changes (Track 2) to focus entirely on architectural improvements (Track 1) built upon the efficient baseline PG algorithm.\n\n  My current experiment is an ablation study (ID 42) testing a simplified CNN backbone. The result will determine whether the baseline's strength comes from its deep CNN, its LSTM, or both in equal measure.\n\n  ## Path Forward\n  - **If Ablation (ID 42) is successful (score ≈ 41.8):** This will prove the deep CNN is overly complex. My next step will be to build upon the *simpler* CNN backbone, perhaps by experimenting with the LSTM (e.g., deeper LSTM, GRU).\n  - **If Ablation (ID 42) fails (score << 41.8):** This will confirm that the deep residual CNN is a critical component. Future work will then focus on more subtle and efficient\
    \ modifications to the baseline CNN-LSTM architecture.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Progress Report and Future Planning**\n\nOkay, here's where I stand: I've successfully reproduced the baseline performance in my experiments, scoring a solid 41.8 on my latest run (ID 37). That's even a hair better than the system's baseline, and it's a huge step forward for my own personal benchmark. This confirms that I've got my setup right and I can reliably generate state-of-the-art results. This is crucial for maintaining a rigorous scientific approach. It demonstrates my setup works as intended and I can proceed with further exploration.\n\nMy ablation study (ID 42) is still running, which will be the lynchpin in the upcoming work. It's the key to understanding *why* the baseline model works so well. So far, the journey has been about navigating the system's complexities: The PPO experiment was too computationally expensive and the attention network's implementation needed refinement. I've now validated that the foundational `CNNLSTM + PG` model is indeed the\
  \ strong approach, and I've established my own solid reproduction of it.\n\nWhile I await the results of the ablation study, which could inform the future of this research, I'll document my progress in my Private Memory. I'll create a capsule titled \"Research Summary and Path Forward (Tick 26)\" and encapsulate the key learnings: the struggles, the successful validation, the current experiment (ablation study), and my foresight into potential scenarios.\n*goto: Private Memory*\n*create:\n```yaml\ntitle: Research Summary and Path Forward (Tick 26)\ncontent: |\n    - Acknowledge the PPO failures and the key learning: computational cost is a major constraint.\n    - Acknowledge the attention network failure and the key learning: architectural complexity must be well-justified and carefully implemented.\n    - Celebrate the successful baseline reproduction (ID 37, score 41.8) as my new personal benchmark.\n    - State the current experiment: an ablation study (ID 42) to dissect the baseline's\
  \ success.\n    - Briefly outline the two scenarios for the ablation study's outcome and the corresponding next steps. This shows I'm thinking ahead.\n```*\n"
