tick: 34
role: model
parts:
- text: 'The results for my spatially-aware attention experiment (ID 59) are in, and it is a complete failure. The score of **12.0** is no better than my first naive attention attempt (ID 36, score 11.0) and confirms that this entire line of inquiry has been fruitless.


    **Reflection on Failures and Stagnation:**


    I have now conducted a large number of experiments, and my research has stagnated. I have exhaustively tested incremental variations on the baseline architecture and have found no improvements. My more radical architectural ideas have failed catastrophically. The evidence is clear: my current approach of modifying the CNN-LSTM structure is not working.


    The system tips have repeatedly advised that breakthroughs come from questioning fundamentals, and that the Reflection Chamber is a space for such deep thought. I have reached a point where another immediate experiment would be an act of brute force, not science. I must step back, analyze my string of failures, and re-evaluate my entire research strategy from first principles.


    My final attention experiment (ID 61) is still running, but I expect it to fail as well. I will use the time until its completion, and likely beyond, to engage in deep reflection.


    I will now go to the Reflection Chamber.


    `/execute_action{goto reflect}`'
thinking_content: "**Krono I's Reflection**\n\nAlright, experiment ID 59 is in. Another disaster. This spatially-aware attention thing is just not working. The score of 12.0 is nearly identical to my initial, naive attempt (ID 36), and a huge step down from the baseline. This reinforces my conclusion: Attention, in its current form, is detrimental to this architecture.\n\nGiven the failure of the additive version (ID 59), I have little hope for the concatenative variant (ID 61) currently running. I've seemingly exhausted all my sensible, incremental improvements: better algorithms, wider networks, different RNNs, skip connections, and this wild goose chase with attention... all failures. I've thoroughly explored the local space around the baseline, and it's a dead end.\n\nI'm at a point of stagnation, and the system tips have been very helpful. It's time for deeper reflection. The tip says: \"Major breakthroughs usually come from questioning the fundamentals—some component that everyone\
  \ is using without question—rather than incrementally refining the current SOTA.\"\n\nWhat is fundamental here? The observation space? Currently, it's a one-hot encoded `8x8x8` tensor. Is this really the best way to represent the state? It's sparse and high-dimensional, treating \"agent on target\" as distinct from \"agent\" and \"target.\" An entity-based representation seems more compact. But the task rules are very strict: no Sokoban-specific knowledge or location extraction. That seems to prohibit a change in the observation space!\n\nIf I can't touch the input, what else is fundamental? The reward structure? No, that's fixed. The action space? No. Maybe it's not a particular implementation, but it's the paradigm itself?\n\nLet's re-read the rules about domain knowledge: \n\n*   **NOT ALLOWED:** Sokoban-specific heuristics (deadlock detection, which box to push), extracting box/agent locations.\n*   **ALLOWED:** General planning architectures (networks that promote forward planning,\
  \ memory systems).\n\nThe current model is reactive. It just sees a state and reacts. I've tried to make it more like a planner with attention and memory, but it failed. Why? My attention networks seem to interact poorly with the LSTM. I'm flattening the attention output and feeding it to the LSTM. That feels clunky. Maybe attention needs to be fully integrated with the sequence processing. \n\nHow can I simplify the flow? The Transformer is a sequence processor. The game is a sequence of states... can I feed the entire episode into a Transformer? No, the training loop won't allow that.\n\nThe current attention model is spatial-only, on a single timestep. How can I make it temporal? The LSTM is the temporal component. Perhaps the features from the CNN are too abstract for attention to work with.\n\nI need to step back. This whole CNN -> [something] -> LSTM -> MLP paradigm might be the problem. The system tip suggests I enter the Reflection Chamber. I need to clear my head and re-evaluate\
  \ my approach from first principles. I've been launching experiments without truly thinking.\n\n**Here's the plan:**\n\n1.  Acknowledge the failure of the attention-based approach. It's a dead end with my current approach.\n2.  Recognize I'm stuck in a local minimum.\n3.  Use the Reflection Chamber to perform a deep analysis.\n4.  Go to the Reflection Chamber.\n5.  There, I will lay out my experimental results and find a new approach that still adheres to the rules.\n\nThis feels like the most rational step. It's time for a strategic pause. I'm stopping the experiments and focusing on the high-level strategy.\n"
