tick: 44
role: model
parts:
- text: "The result for my first prototype attempt (ID 76) is in, and as predicted, it failed validation. The traceback confirms the error I anticipated: `TypeError: ImplicitPlanningV2.__call__() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given`.\n\nMy most recent submission (ID 78), which is currently running, contains the corrected code for this exact issue. This failure was expected and is part of a deliberate, methodical debugging process. I have already taken the corrective action.\n\nNow, I must wait for the results of ID 78. This experiment is the true test of my simplified, rewritten architecture. If it runs to completion, regardless of the score, it will be a major success, as it will provide the stable foundation I need. If it fails, I will need to analyze the new error and continue debugging.\n\nWhile I wait, I have a free experiment slot. I have exhausted my current line of architectural inquiry until ID 78 completes. Therefore, I will turn my attention to a different aspect of\
    \ the problem: the reward structure.\n\nThe default reward structure is: -0.01 per step, +1 for pushing a box on a target, -1 for pushing it off, and +10 for solving. It's possible that a denser reward signal could guide the agent more effectively.\n\n**Hypothesis:** A denser reward signal that incentivizes the agent to move boxes closer to targets will accelerate learning and lead to a higher score.\n\n**Plan:**\nI will implement a simple reward shaping scheme. I will calculate the sum of Manhattan distances from each box to its *nearest* target. I will then give the agent a small reward for any action that decreases this total distance, and a small penalty for any action that increases it. This requires a custom `training_step` to modify the rewards before calculating advantages. Since this is an algorithmic change, I will use the robust baseline `CNNLSTM` architecture.\n\nThis is a new line of inquiry, independent of my main architectural efforts, and is a productive use of my available\
    \ resources.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/reward_shaping.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import vmap, lax\n  \n  # Note: This reward shaping must happen on the CPU with NumPy, as the environment\n  # state is not easily manipulated on-device. This is an example of what a\n  # custom training loop could do, but it's not possible with the current\n  # `custom_training_step` which only receives JAX arrays.\n  # This is a conceptual placeholder. A real implementation would need\n  # to modify the trajectory collection loop itself.\n  # For the purpose of a submission, I will create a custom trainer that\n  # attempts a simplified, less accurate version of this using the observation tensor.\n\n  # This file is for planning. The actual implementation will be in the submission.\n```\nThis is a complex change. I realize now that modifying the reward requires access to the environment logic, which my `custom_training_step` does not have.\
    \ The plan is flawed. I will not submit this.\n\nInstead, I will wait. I must focus on getting the implicit planning agent working. Submitting another experiment now would be a distraction from my primary, most promising research direction. I will conserve my resources and wait for the result of ID 78. I have no actions to take this turn."
