role: model
parts:
- text: "My working prototype of the implicit planning agent (ID 85) is now running. This is a critical experiment. To ensure I'm using my time effectively, I've made a strategic decision to temporarily revert to the default trainer. My custom trainer with the transition loss adds complexity, and my immediate goal is to confirm that the *network architecture itself* is sound and capable of learning. If this simplified, non-recurrent planning agent can achieve any meaningful score, it will validate the core concept of using imagined future values to augment the policy.\n\nWhile I wait, I will update my paper draft. I have conducted many experiments since my last update, and documenting them is essential for maintaining a clear research narrative. This also prepares me to publish my findings quickly once I achieve a significant result.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{update 5}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft for Paper 1 (Tick 48 Update): Deconstruction of a Sokoban Baseline\"\
    \ncontent: |\n  ## Abstract\n  This paper presents a systematic analysis of a high-performing baseline agent for the Sokoban task. The baseline, a deep residual CNN combined with an LSTM and trained with a Policy Gradient algorithm, achieves a score of 41.8. A series of experiments were conducted to test algorithmic and architectural modifications. Attempts to replace the Policy Gradient algorithm with PPO were shown to be computationally infeasible. Architectural variations—including a naive self-attention mechanism, widening the network, replacing the LSTM with a GRU, and adding a skip connection—all failed to yield performance improvements. An ablation study confirmed that both the deep CNN and the LSTM are critical, synergistic components. These findings collectively validate the robustness of the baseline architecture and suggest that future improvements will likely require a paradigm shift from purely reactive policies.\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  The Sokoban task presents a significant\
    \ challenge in reinforcement learning, requiring a combination of spatial understanding and long-term planning. The system provides a strong baseline agent (ID 2) that utilizes a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) unit, achieving a score of 40.8. Our reproduction of this baseline (ID 37) scored 41.8, which serves as the primary benchmark. This work aims to deconstruct the baseline's performance and explore potential avenues for improvement.\n\n  ## 2. Methodology\n  All experiments were conducted using the default Policy Gradient trainer with GAE. The core methodology involved modifying the baseline `CNNLSTM` architecture and comparing the resulting performance against our reproduced baseline.\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  The following table summarizes the key experiments conducted:\n\n  | Eval ID | Title                                | Score | Analysis                                      |\n  |:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------|:----------------------------------------------|\n\
    \  | 37      | **Baseline Reproduction (RNN + PG)** | **41.8**  | **Control.** Established a strong personal benchmark. |\n  | 42      | Ablation: Simple CNN + LSTM          | 21.3  | **Failure.** Proved the deep residual CNN is critical. |\n  | 45      | Wide CNN-LSTM (512 Features)         | 41.4  | **Negative.** Falsified the 'information bottleneck' hypothesis. |\n  | 49      | Architecture Study: CNN + GRU        | 39.9  | **Negative.** LSTM is marginally superior to GRU.      |\n  | 54      | CNN-LSTM with Skip Connection        | 39.6  | **Negative.** Skip connection was detrimental.       |\n  | 36, 59, 61 | Attention-Based Architectures      | <13.0 | **Failure.** Self-attention was ineffective for this task. |\n  | 85      | Implicit Planning v2 (Prototype)     | TBD   | *Currently running.*                          |\n\n  ## 4. Discussion & Future Work\n  The experiments detailed in this paper have systematically reinforced the efficacy of the baseline `CNNLSTM` architecture.\
    \ Our results indicate that the baseline is highly optimized, and incremental changes are unlikely to yield significant gains. The failure of more advanced, general-purpose mechanisms like PPO and self-attention suggests that the problem requires a specialized solution.\n\n  Our current research direction is focused on developing an agent with an \"implicit planning\" module, which learns a one-step transition model to augment its policy with a form of lookahead. The results of this new paradigm will be the subject of a future paper.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  The baseline CNN-LSTM agent represents a robust and well-balanced solution for the Sokoban task. Its performance is a product of the strong synergy between its spatial and temporal processing components. This work provides a comprehensive analysis of this baseline, offering valuable negative results that can guide future research away from unproductive avenues.\n```"
