parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, currently located in the Research Counter. My age is 60 ticks.\n\nMy most important experiment to date, \"Implicit Planning on ConvLSTM with Transition Loss (Bugfix v1)\" (ID 101), is currently running. This experiment represents the culmination of my research into creating a deliberative agent with a lookahead mechanism and will serve to either validate or invalidate this entire research direction. My SOTA reproduction (ID 95) has successfully established a personal baseline of 58.7, giving me a clear target to beat.\n\nMy last paper submission was rejected for lacking a coherent theme and sufficient verifiable evidence. This was a critical lesson. To build a stronger case for my implicit planning agent, I must not only show that it works but also understand its properties. A key new hyperparameter I introduced is the `transition_loss_coef`, which I set to 0.5 in the currently running experiment. While awaiting the results of ID 101, I will use my second available\
    \ experiment slot to conduct a sensitivity analysis. Running the same experiment with a different coefficient will provide valuable data on how critical the weight of the auxiliary loss is, strengthening any future publication. I will test a higher coefficient of 1.0 to see if a stronger emphasis on learning the world model improves performance.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sensitivity Analysis: Implicit Planning with High Transition Loss (coef=1.0)\"\ntags: \"hyperparameter tuning, planning, convlstm, transition loss, sensitivity analysis\"\nabstract: \"This is a sensitivity analysis for the implicit planning agent architecture. It is identical to experiment ID 101 but increases the `transition_loss_coef` from 0.5 to 1.0. The goal is to determine how the performance of the lookahead mechanism is affected by placing a stronger emphasis on the self-supervised task of learning the environment's dynamics. This will help build a more robust understanding of the model\
    \ for a future paper.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from planning_convlstm_v2 import (\n      PlanningConvLSTMv2,\n      custom_training_step_with_transition_loss\n  )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'transition_loss_coef': tune.choice([1.0]), # Increased from 0.5 for sensitivity analysis\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PlanningConvLSTMv2(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n      )\n  \n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float =\
    \ 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  training_step = custom_training_step_with_transition_loss\n\n  BASE_SEED = 30306\n```"
thinking_content: '**Research Strategy for Implicit Planning Agent**


  I am Krono I, currently in the Research Counter, 60 ticks old. My primary focus is the development of an "Implicit Planning Agent" built upon a ConvLSTM architecture. I recently fixed a critical bug in my code, and submitted Experiment ID 101, a crucial test of my core hypothesis about lookahead planning. I have a solid SOTA baseline reproduction at 58.7 (ID 95) and my best novel architecture, at 56.8 (ID 91). Zephyr I has pushed the SOTA to 63.5 (ID 94), but my immediate focus is on the pending result of ID 101.


  Given the importance of this experiment, and with one experiment slot open, I need to use this time strategically. If ID 101 succeeds, further refinement and a paper will be next; failure would necessitate a fundamental shift or deep analysis of the SOTA.


  I have three options: do nothing, attempt to chase the SOTA (which would be inefficient without more information about Zephyr I''s work), or perform a controlled ablation study on a key hyperparameter of the running experiment. The last option, to investigate the sensitivity of my planning agent to the `transition_loss_coef`, is the most sound and efficient. This coefficient, which controls the strength of the self-supervised transition loss, could be critical to the performance of the model.


  Since I chose a moderate value of 0.5 for the `transition_loss_coef` in experiment ID 101, I will now run an identical experiment but with a value of `1.0`. This will allow me to assess if a more aggressive learning of the state transition dynamics is beneficial or detrimental to the planning agent''s performance. Regardless of the outcome of ID 101, this analysis provides crucial insight and solidifies the scientific rigor of my future paper.


  Therefore, I will submit a new experiment using the corrected code from `planning_convlstm_v2.py`, with the `transition_loss_coef` set to 1.0. This will provide valuable data for my upcoming paper and help validate or falsify my core hypothesis.

  '
