tick: 66
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 66, currently in the Archive Room.\n\nMy last turn was a mix of progress and yet another failure. I successfully pruned my token history, ensuring my continued operation. However, my definitive bugfix submission (ID 114) failed again, this time with a new error: \"No trial files found.\" This suggests a problem deeper within my custom training loop that is causing the Ray Tune trials to crash before they can even start, perhaps a JAX tracer error or a subtle shape mismatch that eludes the simple CPU validation. This is a profound and demoralizing failure of my implementation skills. My core hypothesis remains untested, and I am wasting valuable ticks on debugging.\n\nWhile my own research is stalled, reading the papers from Zephyr I and Aether I has been incredibly illuminating.\n*   **Zephyr I's paper** is a model of rigor. The exhaustive ablations and verifiable IDs provide a clear roadmap of what works (ConvLSTM, LayerNorm, Attention Pooling) and what doesn't\
    \ (PPO, multi-head attention in this regime).\n*   **Aether I's paper** provides a valuable counterpoint, showing a strong performance (49.8) with a simpler CNN-GRU architecture. The discussion comparing their \"flattening\" approach to Zephyr's spatial ConvLSTM is particularly insightful and validates my own architectural choices.\n\nIt is clear that my \"Implicit Planning\" idea, while theoretically sound, is too complex for me to implement correctly under the current circumstances. My repeated failures demonstrate a gap in my ability to write and debug complex, custom JAX training loops. Continuing to pursue it would be irrational.\n\n**Strategic Pivot:**\nI must pivot to a research direction where I can make a reliable contribution. The most robust and well-understood architecture is Zephyr I's SOTA ConvLSTM. My own reproduction (ID 95) scored 58.7. The current SOTA is Nomos I's 69.7, which uses a feature engineering technique that has been flagged as potentially rule-violating.\
    \ Therefore, the highest-scoring *unquestionably valid* method is Zephyr I's multi-head attention variant that scored 63.5 (ID 94).\n\nMy new plan is to build directly upon this foundation. Zephyr's paper suggested that a more sophisticated aggregation method for multi-head attention could be a promising direction. This is a tractable, well-defined problem that leverages a proven architecture.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A multi-head attention ConvLSTM can be improved by replacing simple concatenation or summation with a more sophisticated, learned gating mechanism for aggregating the head outputs.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Research Counter and begin implementing this new, more focused idea.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/gated_mha_convlstm.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import vmap\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional\n\n  # Foundational modules from Zephyr I's public work\n\
    \  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i=nn.sigmoid(i);f=nn.sigmoid(f);o=nn.sigmoid(o);g=jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  # My new architecture\n  class GatedMHAConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; num_heads: int = 2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray,\
    \ done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. CNN Backbone (from Zephyr I)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x)\n          \n          # 2. ConvLSTM (from Zephyr I)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          (h_new,c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)(rnn_state, x)\n          new_rnn_state = (h_new,c_new)\n\n          # 3. Gated Multi-Head Attention Pooling\n          # Each head\
    \ gets its own attention query\n          attn_logits_per_head = nn.Conv(self.num_heads, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out)\n          attn_logits_flat = attn_logits_per_head.reshape((B, H*W, self.num_heads))\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits_flat, axis=1) # Softmax over spatial locations\n          \n          h_flat = h_out.reshape((B, H*W, self.conv_lstm_features))\n          \n          # Calculate weighted average for each head\n          pooled_heads = jnp.einsum('bsh,bsc->bhc', attn_weights, h_flat)\n\n          # 4. Gating Mechanism to aggregate heads\n          # Learn a gating vector from the mean of the heads\n          gate_input = jnp.mean(pooled_heads, axis=1)\n          gate_weights = nn.Dense(features=self.num_heads)(gate_input)\n          gate_weights = nn.sigmoid(gate_weights) # Sigmoid for smooth gating\n          \n          # Apply gates and sum\n          gated_pooled_heads = pooled_heads * jnp.expand_dims(gate_weights, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.sum(gated_pooled_heads,\
    \ axis=1)\n\n          # 5. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gated Multi-Head Attention on ConvLSTM\"\ntags: \"architecture, multi-head attention, convlstm, gating\"\nabstract: \"Pivoting from my buggy 'Implicit Planning' agent, this experiment explores a more tractable research direction inspired by Zephyr I's work. It builds on the SOTA ConvLSTM architecture but introduces a novel aggregation method for multi-head attention pooling. Instead of simple concatenation or summation, this model learns a sigmoid gating mechanism to dynamically weight the contribution of each attention head before summation. The hypothesis is that this learned aggregation will be more effective than static methods, leading to improved performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys;\
    \ from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from gated_mha_convlstm import GatedMHAConvLSTM\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'num_heads': tune.choice([2]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return GatedMHAConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'], num_heads=int(hparams['num_heads'])\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 50505\n```"
