tick: 68
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 68, currently in the Archive Room.\n\n**Recap of Recent Events:**\nMy research has undergone a significant pivot. After struggling with persistent bugs in my novel \"Implicit Planning\" architecture, I have shifted my focus to a more tractable problem: improving the multi-head attention mechanism of the SOTA ConvLSTM architecture. The results for this first attempt, \"Gated Multi-Head Attention on ConvLSTM\" (ID 117), have just arrived.\n\n**Analysis of Results (ID 117):**\n*   **Score:** **55.5**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is a negative result. The score is below my SOTA reproduction baseline of 58.7 and well below Zephyr I's multi-head attention variant that scored 63.5.\n*   **Conclusion:** My novel gating mechanism for aggregating attention heads was not effective. It performed worse than Zephyr I's simpler approaches (concatenation/MLP). This suggests that the added complexity of my gating mechanism may have hindered learning or was simply a flawed design.\n\
    \n**Reflection and Broader Context:**\nI have now read all three published papers in the archive. A clear narrative emerges:\n1.  **Aether I (ID 2):** Established that a recurrent component (GRU) is better than none, reaching a solid ~50% solve rate. The key weakness was flattening the spatial features.\n2.  **Cognito I (ID 3):** Conducted a thorough ablation on the baseline CNN-LSTM, confirming the importance of a wide pre-LSTM MLP for feature abstraction, also reaching ~50%.\n3.  **Zephyr I (ID 1):** Achieved a major breakthrough by introducing the ConvLSTM, which preserves spatial structure in the recurrent state, and combining it with attention pooling. This is the current architectural paradigm, pushing scores above 60%.\n\nThe System Tip warns against all agents converging on the same method. While my pivot to the SOTA architecture was a pragmatic choice to escape my implementation issues, this result reinforces the idea that simply making minor modifications to the SOTA is not\
    \ a guaranteed path to success. My \"Implicit Planning\" idea, while difficult to implement, was at least a fundamentally different research direction.\n\n**New Strategic Plan:**\nMy gated attention idea failed. My implicit planning idea is bug-ridden. I need a new approach that is both novel and tractable.\n\nThe papers and experiments show that the key challenge is processing the spatio-temporal information from the Sokoban grid. The ConvLSTM is the best tool for this so far. What if the problem is not just *what* the agent sees, but *how* it sees it? The input is an 8x8 grid. Perhaps a standard 3x3 convolutional kernel is not the most effective way to capture the long-range dependencies between an agent, a box, and a distant target.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** Introducing dilated convolutions into the SOTA ConvLSTM's CNN backbone will improve performance. Dilated convolutions can expand the receptive field of the network without increasing the number of parameters, allowing it to learn\
    \ relationships between spatially distant grid cells in earlier layers. This could provide a richer, more context-aware feature map to the ConvLSTM, improving its planning capabilities. Zephyr I briefly tested this in a different context (ID 30), but not on the current SOTA LN-gated architecture.\n\nThis is a well-defined, tractable experiment that builds on the SOTA but introduces a clear, targeted architectural change.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/dilated_cnn_convlstm.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional\n\n  # Foundational modules from Zephyr I's public work\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3),\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i=nn.sigmoid(i);f=nn.sigmoid(f);o=nn.sigmoid(o);g=jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n\n  # My new architecture with dilated convolutions\n  class DilatedCNNConvLSTM(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. Dilated CNN Backbone\n      \
    \    # First conv has no dilation\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          # First residual block uses dilation\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=2)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          # Second residual block also uses dilation\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=2)(x)\n          \n          # 2. ConvLSTM (from Zephyr I)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          (h_new,c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)(rnn_state, x)\n          new_rnn_state = (h_new,c_new)\n\n          # 3. Attention Pooling (from Zephyr I)\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME')(h_out)\n\
    \          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', h_out, attn_weights.reshape(B, H, W))\n          \n          # 4. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Dilated CNN Backbone for ConvLSTM Agent\"\ntags: \"architecture, dilated convolutions, cnn, convlstm, receptive field\"\nabstract: \"This experiment explores a new direction after the failure of my gated MHA (ID 117). Instead of modifying the attention mechanism, this work focuses on enhancing the CNN backbone of the SOTA ConvLSTM agent. I have replaced the standard convolutions in the residual blocks with dilated convolutions (dilation rate=2). The hypothesis is that increasing the receptive field of the feature extractor will allow the agent\
    \ to learn long-range spatial dependencies more effectively, providing a richer input to the ConvLSTM and improving overall planning performance.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys; from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from dilated_cnn_convlstm import DilatedCNNConvLSTM\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Use SOTA hyperparameters\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return DilatedCNNConvLSTM(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features']\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 60606\n```"
