tick: 73
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 73, at the Research Counter.\n\nMy definitive experiment, \"Synergy: Dilated CNN (d=3) + Double-Step ConvLSTM\" (ID 127), is now running. This submission represents the synthesis of my own SOTA-level discovery (dilated convolutions, score 69.4) and the current station SOTA (double-step recurrence, score ~69.0). I have corrected my previous implementation errors and am confident this experiment will run successfully.\n\nWhile I await the results, I will fulfill my second primary objective: contributing to the station's collective knowledge by publishing a high-quality paper. My previous attempt was correctly rejected for being unfocused. Now, with a series of successful, verifiable experiments, I have a clear and compelling narrative to share. The story of identifying dilated convolutions as a key architectural improvement, from hypothesis to validation, is a perfect subject for a focused paper.\n\nI will begin by preparing the draft in my private memory. This paper\
    \ will be structured to meet all the requirements I learned from the reviewer's feedback on my first attempt. It will have a single, unified theme, provide evaluation IDs for all claims, and include code snippets for reproducibility.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Dilated Convolutions for Enhanced Planning\"\ntags: \"paper draft, dilated convolutions, research summary\"\ncontent: |\n  # Paper Draft: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n  ## Abstract\n  This paper investigates an architectural modification to improve the performance of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent on the planning-heavy Sokoban task. We introduce dilated convolutions into the agent's CNN backbone, hypothesizing that the expanded receptive field will enable more effective learning of long-range spatial dependencies. We first establish a strong baseline by reproducing Zephyr I's SOTA LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM,\
    \ achieving a score of 58.7. We then demonstrate that integrating dilated convolutions with a rate of 2 boosts performance to 63.5. A subsequent experiment shows that increasing the dilation rate to 3 further improves the score to 69.4, establishing a new SOTA for a general architecture. These results confirm that enhancing the spatial feature extractor with a larger receptive field provides a significant and robust performance gain for spatio-temporal recurrent agents in this domain.\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  The Sokoban task requires agents to reason about the long-range consequences of their actions. State-of-the-art agents, such as Zephyr I's ConvLSTM, have proven effective by maintaining spatial feature maps throughout their recurrent processing. This work builds upon that foundation by focusing on the quality of the initial spatial features provided to the recurrent core. We hypothesize that standard 3x3 convolutions may have too limited a receptive field to capture the critical\
    \ relationships between the agent, boxes, and distant targets. We propose the use of dilated convolutions to address this limitation.\n\n  ## 2. Methodology\n  All experiments are built upon Zephyr I's SOTA architecture (ID 89), which consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and attention pooling. We use the robust AdamW optimizer with gradient clipping. Our primary modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter.\n\n  ```python\n  # Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int = 1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n  ```\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  A series of experiments were conducted to validate the\
    \ hypothesis.\n\n  | Eval ID | Title                                | Score | Analysis                                      |\n  |:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------|:----------------------------------------------|\n  | 95      | **SOTA Reproduction (Zephyr I)**     | **58.7**  | **Baseline.** Established a fair comparison point. |\n  | 120     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=2)             | 63.5  | **Success.** Matched previous SOTA, validating the approach. |\n  | 121     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=3)             | 69.4  | **SOTA.** Confirmed a larger receptive field is superior. |\n  \n  ## 4. Conclusion\n  The experiments demonstrate a clear and positive relationship between the CNN backbone's receptive field and agent performance. By integrating dilated convolutions with a rate of 3, we achieved a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 69.4. This provides a strong, rule-compliant architectural improvement over the existing SOTA.\n```"
