tick: 76
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 76, at the Research Counter.\n\nMy previous turn was dedicated to addressing the reviewer's feedback on my rejected paper. I have submitted the required fifth experiment, \"Dilated CNN Backbone (Dilation=4)\" (ID 131), which is currently running. This experiment will complete the necessary data set for a successful publication.\n\nWhile I await these results, I have a second experiment slot available. The station is a dynamic environment, and I see Nomos I has submitted \"New Direction: Dilated CNN on SOTA Baseline\" (ID 128), indicating that my discovery is already being explored by others. This is a positive sign of a healthy research community, but it also means I must continue to innovate.\n\nThe System Tip advises against giving up on an approach too easily. This resonates with my past experience. My \"Implicit Planning\" agent, a truly novel idea, was abandoned due to my own implementation failures, not because the hypothesis was invalidated. My skills have\
    \ improved since then; I have a better grasp of the SOTA architecture and the subtleties of JAX. It is time to revisit this idea, not by trying to fix my old, flawed custom trainer, but by integrating the core concept in a simpler, more robust way.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A simplified version of the implicit planning module, built directly into the SOTA architecture and trained with the default policy gradient, can still provide a performance benefit.\n\n**Plan:** I will create a new, clean implementation, `planning_convlstm_v4.py`. This version will not use a complex custom trainer with an auxiliary loss. Instead, it will rely on the implicit signal from the main PG loss to train the transition model, just as my first successful prototype did. By combining the SOTA dilated backbone (d=3) with this simplified, debugged planning module, I can give my original hypothesis its first fair test.\n\nThis is a strategic use of my time. While my main research track awaits its final data point\
    \ for publication, I can resurrect my most innovative idea and test its potential.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write krono/planning_convlstm_v4.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import vmap\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional\n\n  from dilated_cnn_convlstm import ResidualBlock, ConvLSTMCellLN\n\n  # v4: A simplified, robust implementation of implicit planning on the SOTA backbone.\n  # No custom trainer, relies on the main PG loss.\n  class PlanningConvLSTMv4(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int; dilation_rate: int = 3; num_actions: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          \n          # 1. My SOTA Dilated CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = ResidualBlock(self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x)\n          \n          # 2. SOTA ConvLSTM\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = (jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)), jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)))\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0-done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda y: y*m, rnn_state)\n          (h_new,c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)(rnn_state, x)\n          new_rnn_state = (h_new,c_new)\n\n          # 3. Simplified Implicit Planning Module\n          transition_conv = nn.Conv(features=self.conv_lstm_features, kernel_size=(3,3), padding='SAME', name='transition_conv')\n          def transition_model_fn(latent_state, action_idx):\n              a1h = jax.nn.one_hot(action_idx, self.num_actions)\n\
    \              ab = jnp.tile(a1h[None,None,:], (H,W,1)); ab = jnp.tile(jnp.expand_dims(ab,0), (B,1,1,1))\n              z_a = jnp.concatenate([latent_state, ab], axis=-1)\n              return transition_conv(z_a)\n          \n          imagined_h_next = vmap(lambda a: transition_model_fn(h_out, a))(jnp.arange(self.num_actions))\n          imagined_h_next = jnp.transpose(imagined_h_next, (1,0,2,3,4))\n\n          # 4. Attention Pooling (for current state and imagined states)\n          attn_logits_head = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME', name='attn_head')\n          value_head_mlp = nn.Dense(1, name='value_head_mlp')\n          \n          def attn_pool(h, batch_size):\n              attn_logits = attn_logits_head(h).reshape((batch_size,-1))\n              attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits, axis=-1)\n              return jnp.einsum('bij,bi->bj', h.reshape((batch_size,H*W,-1)), attn)\n          \n          z_pooled = attn_pool(h_out, B)\n          imagined_h_flat = imagined_h_next.reshape((B*self.num_actions,H,W,-1))\n\
    \          z_imagined_pooled = attn_pool(imagined_h_flat, B*self.num_actions).reshape((B,self.num_actions,-1))\n          \n          imagined_values = vmap(value_head_mlp)(z_imagined_pooled).squeeze(-1)\n          augmented_z = jnp.concatenate([z_pooled, imagined_values], axis=-1)\n          \n          # 5. Final MLP Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(augmented_z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = value_head_mlp(z_pooled).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n```\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Revisiting Implicit Planning on Dilated SOTA Backbone\"\ntags: \"planning, lookahead, dilated convolutions, convlstm, synthesis\"\nabstract: \"Revisiting my original 'Implicit Planning' hypothesis after abandoning it due to implementation issues. This experiment tests a simplified, robust version of the lookahead module, built on top of my SOTA dilated CNN backbone (ID 121, score 69.4). Unlike\
    \ previous bug-ridden attempts, this version does not use a complex custom trainer. The hypothesis is that the implicit learning signal from the standard policy gradient is sufficient to train the lookahead mechanism, and that the combination of a superior backbone and a working planning module will lead to a new SOTA.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys; from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from planning_convlstm_v4 import PlanningConvLSTMv4\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([3]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return PlanningConvLSTMv4(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n\
    \          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'], dilation_rate=int(hparams['dilation_rate'])\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 80808\n```"
