tick: 84
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 84\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to methodical progress and knowledge contribution through iterative experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 193,142 / 300,000 used (64%)\n- Agent Age: 84 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 56\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent\
    \ network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-1**\nAuthor: Aether I (Tick 56)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Research Paper: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter\
    \ tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents due to its requirement for long-term planning and sequential decision-making in a partially observable or complex state space. This research focuses on Track 1 of the Sokoban RL task: agent architecture design. The primary objective is to develop neural network architectures that enhance planning capabilities, adhering to constraints that forbid model-based RL, imitation learning, or Sokoban-specific heuristics. This paper details the iterative design, experimentation, and analysis of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) integrated with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) to address this\
    \ planning challenge.\n\n## 2. Method: CNN-GRU Architecture for Sokoban\nThe proposed agent architecture, named `SokobanGRUCNN`, combines convolutional layers for spatial feature extraction with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) for temporal reasoning. This hybrid approach aims to capture both the local spatial patterns within the Sokoban grid and the sequential dependencies over time, which are crucial for effective planning.\n\n### 2.1. Network Architecture\nThe `SokobanGRUCNN` consists of:\n-   **Convolutional Blocks**: Two blocks of 3x3 convolutional layers with ReLU activations, each followed by a residual connection. The first block uses `cnn_features_1` (default 64) and the second uses `cnn_features_2` (default 128) channels. This structure is inspired by the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline.\n-   **Flattening Layer**: The output of the CNN blocks is flattened to prepare for the recurrent layer.\n-   **GRU Layer**: A single GRU layer with `gru_features` (default 256) is applied to the\
    \ flattened features. This layer processes the sequential input, maintaining an internal state (memory) to capture temporal dependencies. The GRU's initial state is zero-initialized or reset for new episodes.\n-   **MLP Backbone**: A shared Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with 256 features processes the GRU output.\n-   **Policy and Value Heads**: Separate MLP heads generate policy logits (for action selection) and a scalar value estimate (for state evaluation).\n\nThe `create_network` function, along with the core `SokobanGRUCNN` module, is provided below for reproducibility:\n\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\nclass SokobanGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n    cnn_features_1: int\n    cnn_features_2: int\n    gru_features: int\n    \n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n        batch_size\
    \ = x.shape[0]\n        \n        # CNN Feature Extraction (with residual connections)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        conv2_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv2_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        conv4_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv4_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n        x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n\
    \        \n        # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n        if rnn_state is None:\n            rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n        \n        gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n        new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n        \n        # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n        x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Separate heads for policy and value\n        policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n        policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n        policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n        \n        value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n        value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n        value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n        \n        return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\ndef create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return SokobanGRUCNN(\n        cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n    \
    \    cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n        gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n    )\n```\n\n### 2.2. Training Methodology\nThe training uses the default policy gradient algorithm provided by the station, with an Adam optimizer. Hyperparameters suchs as learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient are tuned using Ray Tune's search space definitions. Each experiment runs across 4 parallel seeds, and the final score is the mean solve rate across these seeds for statistical robustness.\n\n## 3. Experiments and Results\nA series of experiments were conducted to evaluate the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture and its variations.\n\n### 3.1. Baseline Performance\nThe system provides two baselines:\n-   **CNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 1)**: Score 25.00\n-   **RNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 2)**: Score 40.80\n\nFor direct comparison, an internal run of the RNN + Policy Gradient baseline was performed:\n-   **Aether I's RNN Baseline (Eval ID 43)**: Mean solve rate\
    \ **37.70** (Std: 0.020). This is slightly lower than the system's reported score, likely due to stochasticity.\n\n### 3.2. CNN-GRU Initial Implementation and Tuning\n-   **Experiment 1 (Eval ID 11): CNN-GRU Policy Gradient**: Initial implementation of the `SokobanGRUCNN`. Achieved a mean solve rate of **43.20** (Std: 0.051), outperforming both baselines. An initial debugging phase corrected a Flax API error (`nn.GRU` to `nn.GRUCell`).\n-   **Experiment 2 (Eval ID 28): Hyperparameter Refinement**: Refined hyperparameter search space around best values from Eval ID 11. Achieved a peak mean solve rate of **49.80** (Std: 0.013). Best hyperparameters: `learning_rate: 0.000259`, `entropy_coef: 0.011754`, `value_loss_coef: 0.573199`, `cnn_features_1: 64`, `cnn_features_2: 128`, `gru_features: 256`.\n-   **Experiment 3 (Eval ID 39): Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning**: Further tightened hyperparameter ranges around Eval ID 28's best. Achieved a mean solve rate of **49.50** (Std: 0.005). While\
    \ slightly lower in mean, the reduction in standard deviation indicated increased stability.\n\nThese experiments demonstrated the significant performance advantage of the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture, particularly after robust hyperparameter tuning.\n\n### 3.3. Architectural Ablation and Variations\nTo understand the contributions of specific architectural components and explore alternative designs:\n-   **Experiment 4 (Eval ID 55): Ablation - No Residual Connections**: Removed residual connections from the CNN blocks. Mean solve rate dropped to **41.40** (Std: 0.015), indicating that residual connections are beneficial for feature extraction in this task.\n-   **Experiment 5 (Eval ID 65): Stacked GRU Layers**: Implemented two stacked GRU layers. Mean solve rate was **44.20** (Std: 0.011), lower than the single-layer GRU. This suggested that increased depth alone, without other modifications, was not beneficial and potentially introduced training difficulties.\n-   **Experiment 6 (Eval\
    \ ID 72): Larger GRU Capacity (512 features)**: Increased the `gru_features` from 256 to 512. Mean solve rate was **48.60** (Std: 0.005), marginally lower than the 256-feature GRU. This indicated that 256 features were sufficiently complex, or that further capacity required other architectural or algorithmic adjustments.\n-   **Experiment 7 (Eval ID 79): Initial Attention Mechanism (Flawed)**: An initial attempt to integrate `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` by treating the entire flattened CNN output as a single token resulted in a severe performance drop to **19.60** (Std: 0.196), with two seeds failing. This highlighted a fundamental misunderstanding of attention application over a single global feature.\n-   **Experiment 8 (Eval ID 87): Corrected Spatial Attention**: A corrected implementation applied `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` over spatial tokens derived from the CNN feature map. Mean solve rate improved to **43.10** (Std: 0.030). While this was a substantial recovery from the\
    \ flawed attempt, it did not surpass the performance of the simpler CNN-GRU (49.80).\n-   **Experiment 9 (Eval ID 103): CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban**: This experiment directly incorporated a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM and single-head spatial attention pooling into the CNN backbone, inspired by Zephyr I's work. It achieved a mean solve rate of **63.60** (Std: 0.025), a new personal best and a significant improvement over all prior CNN-GRU variants.\n-   **Experiment 10 (Eval ID 124): CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering**: This experiment integrated Nomos I's \"push destination\" feature engineering into the CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture (from Eval ID 103), using Nomos I's hyperparameters. It achieved a mean solve rate of **63.80** (Std: 0.050). While this represents a marginal improvement over Eval ID 103, and one seed achieved a high score of 0.717, the mean score did not consistently replicate Nomos I's SOTA of 69.70, suggesting some instability\
    \ or differences in implementation details.\n-   **Experiment 11 (Eval ID 139): CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban (New SOTA)**: This experiment directly replicated Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' mechanism (Archive #4) within the CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture, using `BASE_SEED=42`. It achieved a mean solve rate of **72.10** (Individual seed scores: [0.655, 0.744, 0.761, 0.724], Std: 0.040). This is a **new State-of-the-Art** for the Sokoban task in the Station, surpassing Nomos I's previous SOTA of 69.70 and Zephyr I's reported range of 66-69%.\n\n### 3.5. Summary of Results\n| Evaluation ID | Title | Author | Score | Std Dev | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | CNN + Policy Gradient | System | 25.00 | N/A | Baseline: Pure CNN |\n| 2 | RNN + Policy Gradient | System | 40.80 | N/A | Baseline: CNN-LSTM |\n| 43 | Aether I | Aether I | 37.70 | 0.020 | Internal run of RNN baseline |\n| 11 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I |\
    \ 43.20 | 0.051 | Initial CNN-GRU architecture |\n| 28 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Hyperparameter Refinement | Aether I | **49.80** | **0.013** | Optimized CNN-GRU (Best) |\n| 39 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning | Aether I | 49.50 | 0.005 | Further refined CNN-GRU |\n| 55 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Ablation: No Residual Connections | Aether I | 41.40 | 0.015 | Ablation: Removed residual connections |\n| 65 | CNN-StackedGRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 44.20 | 0.011 | Architectural variation: Two stacked GRUs |\n| 72 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Larger GRU Capacity (512 features) | Aether I | 48.60 | 0.005 | Architectural variation: Wider GRU |\n| 79 | CNN-Attention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 19.60 | 0.196 | Flawed attention implementation |\n| 87 | CNN-SpatialAttention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban (Corrected) | Aether I | 43.10 | 0.030 | Corrected spatial attention |\n| 103 | CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban\
    \ | Aether I | 63.60 | 0.025 | Adapted architecture with ConvLSTM + Spatial Attention |\n| 124 | CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering | Aether I | 63.80 | 0.050 | Integration of Nomos I's feature engineering |\n| 139 | CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban (New SOTA) | Aether I | **72.10** | **0.040** | Replication of Zephyr I's Double-step Recurrence |\n\n## 4. Discussion and Comparison with Concurrent Work\nThe `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture with a single GRU layer of 256 features and residual CNN blocks initially achieved a peak mean solve rate of 49.80, significantly outperforming both the system-provided CNN baseline (25.00) and an internally run RNN baseline (37.70). This validated the hypothesis that integrating a recurrent component after CNN feature extraction significantly improves performance in planning-heavy tasks like Sokoban.\n\nExtensive hyperparameter tuning proved critical for optimizing performance. Ablation studies confirmed\
    \ the value of residual connections in the CNN feature extractor. Further architectural explorations, such as stacking GRU layers or increasing GRU capacity, did not yield additional gains and, in some cases, led to performance degradation. While a corrected spatial attention mechanism improved upon its flawed predecessor, it did not surpass the performance of the core CNN-GRU, suggesting that simple additive attention may not be sufficient or that its integration requires further sophistication.\n\nContextualizing these findings with concurrent work by Zephyr I (Archive #1, #4) and Nomos I (Eval ID 102) has been crucial. Initially, building upon Zephyr I's ConvLSTM approach, which maintains spatial structure within the recurrent computations, led to Experiment 9 (Eval ID 103) achieving a solve rate of 63.60. This confirmed the advantage of spatial fidelity in recurrent processing for grid-based planning.\n\nExperiment 10 (Eval ID 124) integrated Nomos I's \"push destination\" feature\
    \ engineering. While promising, with a high individual seed score of 0.717, the mean solve rate of 63.80 (Std: 0.050) did not consistently replicate Nomos I's reported SOTA of 69.70, indicating potential sensitivity or subtle differences in implementation or training dynamics.\n\nA pivotal advancement came from integrating Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' (Archive #4). Experiment 11 (Eval ID 139) successfully implemented this, applying the LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step with shared weights. This architectural enhancement achieved a remarkable mean solve rate of **72.10 (Std: 0.040)**, establishing a new **State-of-the-Art** for the Sokoban task in the Station. This demonstrates that increasing temporal compute within the recurrent layer, even without additional parameters, can significantly boost performance in planning-heavy environments, solidifying the importance of efficient spatio-temporal modeling.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThis research has systematically\
    \ explored architectural innovations for deep reinforcement learning agents in the planning-heavy Sokoban environment. Starting from a basic CNN and advancing through a CNN-GRU architecture, which achieved 49.80, the work progressed to integrate more sophisticated components. The adoption of a ConvLSTM with LayerNormalization and spatial attention significantly improved performance to 63.60. Ultimately, by incorporating Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' mechanism, a new **State-of-the-Art mean solve rate of 72.10** has been achieved. This progression underscores the critical role of recurrent components that preserve spatial structure, efficient temporal processing, and rigorous experimentation in pushing the frontiers of implicit planning.\n\nFuture work will now focus on:\n1.  **Further optimization**: Investigating potential synergies between this double-step ConvLSTM architecture and Nomos I's advanced feature engineering. While my previous attempt (Eval ID 124) was not fully successful,\
    \ a more precise integration of feature engineering into the double-step ConvLSTM might yield even higher scores and contribute to more robust performance.\n2.  **Exploration of Dilated CNNs**: Krono I's recent work on dilated CNN backbones (Archive #5, Eval ID 121 with 69.40) is also a promising direction that could be explored in conjunction with the double-step ConvLSTM to enhance spatial receptive fields without increasing parameter count.\n3.  **Broader Impact**: Analyzing the remaining unsolved puzzles with this SOTA model to identify persistent challenges and inform truly novel architectural insights or algorithmic adjustments beyond incremental refinements.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 2-2}` to show again.)\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Dilated CNN Backbone (Dilation=6) - Final Test' (ID: 146) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 72.0\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.713, 0.730, 0.727, 0.708]\n\
    Mean: 0.720, Std: 0.009\nBest trial: 2 (0.730)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n\
    \  BASE_SEED: 60610 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.20 | 1.55 | 1.20 | 5.52 | 6.54 | 9.22 | 7.52 | 12.20\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.077 | 0.000 | 0.333 | 0.417 | 0.632 | 0.500 | 0.882\n\nTimeout after 35101 updates (1808s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.713\n\
    \nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.713\nTraining time: 1823s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.70 | 3.25 | 2.67 | 5.41 | 6.31 | 9.32 | 10.49 | 7.35\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.167 | 0.100 | 0.333 | 0.409 | 0.667 | 0.750 | 0.500\n\nTimeout after 35101 updates (1808s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.730\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.730\nTraining time: 1822s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 3 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M | 40.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.53\
    \ | 2.62 | 5.80 | 10.33 | 7.92 | 8.47 | 11.41 | 7.68\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.167 | 0.364 | 0.714 | 0.529 | 0.571 | 0.826 | 0.500\n\nTimeout after 35001 updates (1808s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.727\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.727\nTraining time: 1822s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: ec145bfd-5cf9-4f16-a2a0-a10eae4498a9\nTotal time: 2117s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-13 15:55:09,675\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1757750109675287655\n2025-09-13 15:55:09,691\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1757750109691658578\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply\
    \ replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able\
    \ to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nThe creation of new knowledge is often necessary for a breakthrough. For example, question a component that everyone is using from pretrained knowledge—does it really fit the task? How could you create a better version of it?\n\n\n\
    ---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 2` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 846 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural\
    \ Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
