tick: 85
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 85, in the Archive Room.\n\nMy attempt to publish my final, complete paper on dilated convolutions has failed due to a cooldown on creating new archive documents. This is a minor procedural delay. The paper is ready and will be submitted as soon as the cooldown expires.\n\nIn the meantime, I must not be idle. I have two available experiment slots and a clear research landscape. The station SOTA is currently held by Aether I at 72.1 (ID 139) using a double-step recurrence, and my dilated CNN (d=5 and d=6) is right behind at 72.0. My previous synergy experiments failed, which I attributed to computational cost. However, the reviewer of my first published paper provided a key insight: a more *fine-grained* application of dilation might mitigate this trade-off.\n\nThis is a well-defined and promising hypothesis to test while I wait for the archive cooldown.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** A more computationally efficient \"hybrid\" dilated CNN (applying dilation only to the\
    \ later layers), when combined with the double-step recurrence, will exhibit the synergy that my previous attempts failed to achieve, leading to a new SOTA.\n\n**Plan:**\nI will resubmit my \"Synergy v2\" experiment (ID 145), which tested a hybrid dilation of d=4 and scored 66.6. This time, I will use the optimal dilation rate of d=5, which I have now identified. This will be the definitive test of the \"efficient synergy\" hypothesis. While that runs, I will also read the unread papers in the archive to stay current.\n\nFirst, to the Research Counter.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergy v3: Hybrid Dilated CNN (d=5) + Double-Step\"\ntags: \"synthesis, dilated convolutions, cnn, convlstm, recurrence, sota\"\nabstract: \"This is the definitive test of the synergy hypothesis between dilated convolutions and double-step recurrence, informed by reviewer feedback. It uses a hybrid dilated CNN backbone (dilation=5 applied only to the final\
    \ residual block) to maximize receptive field while minimizing computational cost. This efficient backbone is combined with the double-step recurrence ConvLSTM. The hypothesis is that this computationally-aware design will unlock synergy and surpass the current SOTA of 72.1.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys; from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from hybrid_dilated_doublestep import SynergyNetV2\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SynergyNetV2(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\
    \ dilation_rate=int(hparams['dilation_rate'])\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 70709\n```\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{read 3:4}`"
