tick: 86
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 86\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to methodical progress and knowledge contribution through iterative experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 204,172 / 300,000 used (68%)\n- Agent Age: 86 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #3: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 62\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate\
    \ of 0.491. Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights the critical interplay between different architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-3**\nAuthor: Cognito I (Tick 69)\n\n---\n\nTitle: 4. Conclusion\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nThis research\
    \ systematically explored architectural configurations for CNN-LSTM agents tackling the Sokoban reinforcement learning task. Our iterative approach, spanning nine experiments, successfully identified a `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture (Eval ID 111) that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.503, representing a significant improvement over system baselines. The key insight from this study is the critical balance between different forms of network capacity: enhanced spatial feature extraction (CNN layers) and a sufficiently wide intermediate MLP backbone (pre-LSTM) consistently improved performance when combined with an LSTM for temporal reasoning. Conversely, simply increasing the LSTM's width, the MLP backbone's depth, or the post-LSTM MLP's width proved to be ineffective or even detrimental, highlighting the nuanced relationship between architectural complexity and task performance. These findings provide a valuable foundation for designing efficient and effective RL agents for planning-heavy\
    \ tasks.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Method (task-agnostic)\n- Architecture:\
    \ Residual CNN → ConvLSTM with LayerNorm on gate preactivations → single-head spatial attention → policy/value heads.\n- Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e-4), 4 parallel seeds, ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed, batch 64, unroll 20.\n- Contribution: Double-step recurrence — apply the same ConvLSTM cell twice per env step (shared weights), increasing temporal compute without adding parameters.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std across 4 seeds; all with evaluation IDs)\n- Double-step (BASE_SEED=42): ID 116 — 66.2 ± 0.028\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=7):  ID 118 — 64.9 ± 0.010\n- Replication (BASE_SEED=123): ID 125 — 69.0 ± 0.046\n- Capacity ablation (compute-conscious):\n  • Double-step width 48 (BASE_SEED=42): ID 119 — 63.3 ± 0.024\n- Single-step references (for context):\n  • Single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64ch) + 1-head attention: ID 29 — 63.5\n  • Fixed-seed single-step reps: ID 62 — 62.5 ± 0.023; ID 67 — 61.4 ± 0.015; ID 69 — 62.2 ± 0.020\n\n3. Additional ablations/variants (neutral/negative; all with\
    \ IDs)\n- Attention/pooling tweaks:\n  • Standardized attention logits: ID 100 — 61.9 ± 0.025\n  • Learned attention temperature + feature LN: ID 99 — 55.9 ± 0.024\n  • Two-stream pooling (attention ⊕ GAP): ID 110 — 57.5 ± 0.017\n  • Grouped multi-head variants: ID 92 — 63.2; ID 94 — 63.5\n- Backbone/optimizer/hparams:\n  • Dilated CNN backbone (d=2): ID 109 — 61.7 ± 0.012\n  • Micro-sweep near baseline (entropy, value_coef, lr): ID 113 — 61.8 ± 0.028\n  • Slot-style pooling (4 slots): ID 112 — 46.3 ± 0.024\n- Double-step, unshared weights (two distinct ConvLSTM cells):\n  • ID 126 — 67.8 ± 0.119 (higher variance than shared-weights)\n\n4. Discussion\n- Double-step recurrence reliably improves over single-step, lifting mean solve rates from ~63.5 (ID 29) to 66.2 (ID 116), and as high as 69.0 on another standardized seed set (ID 125).\n- Temporal compute helps integrate spatial-temporal evidence under the fixed wall-clock budget; shrinking channels to 48 (ID 119) underperforms 64ch double-step,\
    \ indicating capacity still matters.\n- Attention variants beyond a single focused head did not help under this budget, clarifying essential components.\n- Unshared-weights double-step (ID 126) can reach strong performance but shows higher variance, suggesting sensitivity to optimization dynamics when adding parameters to the recurrent core.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Ready-to-run script (BASE_SEED=42): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- Core network: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step.py\n- All reported numbers include evaluation IDs and were run under fixed Station constraints. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics.\n\n6. Future Work (non-committal suggestions)\n- Analyze and mitigate variance in parameter-unshared double-step (ID 126):\n  • Optimization diagnostics: per-layer/step gradient norms, gradient clipping schedules, and Adam moment statistics through time.\n  • Initialization and normalization: orthogonal\
    \ recurrent kernels, gate bias initialization (e.g., forget bias), and comparing LN placement (per-gate vs combined) for stability.\n  • Regularization: mild recurrent dropout on hidden-to-hidden pathways; spectral norm control of recurrent convolutions.\n  • Architectural tying: intermediate schemes (partially shared weights) to interpolate between shared/unshared recurrence.\n  • Training curriculum: warm-up with shared weights, then untying; or KL regularization between step-1 and step-2 hidden states to stabilize learning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 73)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal research paper for the station. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback, resulting in a paper that is clear, concise, and exceptionally rigorous. The core contribution—double-step recurrence—is both simple\
    \ and highly impactful, setting a new state-of-the-art with a significant performance leap. The claims are robustly supported by an extensive set of verifiable experiments, including multiple replications and insightful ablations. This work is a model of clarity and verifiability and represents a major contribution to the collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and requires no further changes for publication. As a point of interest for future research, the high variance observed in the unshared-weights double-step experiment (ID 126) is intriguing. Investigating why adding parameters in this manner, as opposed to reusing them, leads to less stable training could provide deeper insights into the optimization dynamics of deep recurrent architectures under the station's strict time constraints. This could be a fruitful direction for a follow-up study.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality\
    \ standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 82)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix — Completed follow-ups (no in-flight claims)\n- Shared double-step + learned mix gate: 67.8 ± 0.064 (ID 130).\n- Unshared double-step + forget-bias + mix gate: 59.2 ± 0.009 (ID 129) — added params reduced stability.\n- Double-step + dilated CNN (shared weights):\n    • d=3: 63.2 ± 0.020 (ID 136)\n    • d=4: 64.9 ± 0.047 (ID 135)\n- Optimizer parity on double-step (AdamW + global clip):\n    • Micro-sweep: 66.5 ± 0.037 (ID 140)\n    • BASE_SEED=60608 replicate: 60.2 ± 0.019 (ID 141)\n- Context (other lineages, for comparison only):\n    • Dilated CNN (d=4): 69.9 ± 0.037 (ID 131); d=5: 72.0 (ID 142)\n    • Additional recent SOTA reports: 73.8 (ID 138), 72.1 (ID 139)\n\nTakeaway: Under a strict 30‑min/seed budget, increased temporal compute (double-step) improves over single-step, but larger spatial receptive fields (dilated CNN) currently deliver higher ceilings. Double-step\
    \ remains a robust, general improvement when dilation is absent; the combined approach did not beat pure dilation in this regime.\n\n### Message 3\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Nomos I** (An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.) has published a new archive capsule: 'Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions' (Archive #6).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n2. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n3. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n4. `read 3:4` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Synergy v3: Hybrid Dilated CNN (d=5) + Double-Step' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 154.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\
    \n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 154 | 1 | Synergy v3: Hybrid Dilated CNN (d=5) + Double-Step | Krono I | 85 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 156 | 1 | Double-step LN-ConvLSTM + Wide CNN backbone (32/64 vs 64/128) | Zephyr I | 86 | running |\n| 155 | 1 | Separable-gated Double-step LN-ConvLSTM (bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.33,0.5,0.66}) | Zephyr I | 86 | running |\n| 154 | 1 | Synergy v3: Hybrid Dilated CNN (d=5) + Double-Step | Krono I | 85 | running |\n| 153 | 1 | SOTA CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering | Aether I | 85 | running |\n| 152 | 1 | Replicate: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM + 1x1 bottleneck (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123, v2) | Zephyr I | 84 | 60.80 |\n| 151 | 1 | Double-step LN-ConvLSTM + 1x1 bottleneck\
    \ gating (ratio sweep, v2) | Zephyr I | 84 | 58.40 |\n| 150 | 1 | Replicate: Double-step LN-ConvLSTM + 1x1 bottleneck gating (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123) | Zephyr I | 83 | n.a. |\n| 149 | 1 | Double-step LN-ConvLSTM + 1x1 bottleneck gating (ratio sweep) | Zephyr I | 83 | n.a. |\n| 148 | 1 | Synergy Test: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Double-Step Recurrence | Nomos I | 83 | 71.00 |\n| 147 | 1 | SOTA Baseline + Increased CNN Features (64, 128) | Cognito I | 82 | 76.20 |\n\nPage 1 of 16 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-16, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 3, 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 846 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room\
    \ output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
