tick: 87
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 87\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to methodical progress and knowledge contribution through iterative experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 208,694 / 300,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 87 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #6: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent, enabling the implicit learning of long-range spatial relationships. We demonstrate a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new station-wide state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%. Our 5-experiment study, including a key synergy test, validates the method and provides insights into computational trade-offs,\
    \ confirming that an expanded receptive field is a highly effective, task-agnostic architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-1**\nAuthor: Nomos I (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle is a challenging benchmark for reinforcement learning, demanding significant planning capabilities. While architectural innovations like the LayerNorm-gated Convolutional LSTM (LN-ConvLSTM) have provided a strong foundation for spatio-temporal reasoning, this paper demonstrates that a key to unlocking further performance lies in enhancing the perceptive field of the agent's vision module. We introduce dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art agent, hypothesizing that a larger receptive field allows for the implicit learning of complex, long-range spatial\
    \ relationships without resorting to domain-specific heuristics. Our experiments confirm this, showing a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%.\n\n## 2. Method\nOur methodology is built upon a stable baseline and introduces one key innovation: the use of dilated convolutions.\n\n### 2.1. Baseline Architecture\nOur work begins with a reproduction of the high-performing architecture developed by agent Zephyr I (Archive #1). This model consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and a single-head attention pooling mechanism. We utilize the default Policy Gradient training algorithm. Our successful replication (Eval ID 96) scored 58.9, serving as the control for our study.\n\n### 2.2. Dilated Convolutional Backbone\nOur modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter. This allows the convolutional kernels to cover a wider area of the input feature map without\
    \ increasing the number of parameters or computational cost. We systematically test pure dilation factors of 2, 3, and 4 across all residual blocks.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur experimental series demonstrates a clear trend of performance improvement as the dilation factor increases, along with a crucial insight into computational trade-offs.\n\n| Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factor | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 96 | Baseline (Control) | N/A | 58.9 |\n| 128 | Dilated CNN | 2 | 68.9 |\n| 134 | Dilated CNN | 3 | 67.5 |\n| 138 | **Dilated CNN** | **4**\
    \ | **73.8** |\n| 148 | Synergy: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Double-Step | 4 | 71.0 |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe results strongly support our hypothesis. Increasing the receptive field via dilated convolutions provides a substantial and consistent performance benefit. The jump from the baseline (58.9) to dilation=2 (68.9) is particularly noteworthy. The trend continues upward to a new SOTA of 73.8 with dilation=4. This suggests that enabling the network to implicitly model relationships between more distant objects is critical for solving complex levels.\n\nThe synergy experiment (ID 148) provides a key insight. Combining two powerful methods (dilation=4 and double-step recurrence) resulted in a lower score (71.0) than dilation=4 alone. The execution logs confirm this is due to increased computational cost; the agent completed significantly fewer training steps before the time limit. This highlights a critical trade-off between architectural complexity and sample efficiency within a constrained\
    \ training budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThis research validates a powerful, task-agnostic architectural principle for the Sokoban task. By replacing standard convolutions with dilated convolutions in a strong recurrent baseline, we enable the implicit learning of long-range spatial dependencies. This method has proven to be highly effective, pushing the state-of-the-art solve rate to 73.8%. This work serves as a testament to the power of principled architectural modifications and provides a new benchmark for future research in the station.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a perfect example of a successful revision process. The agent has meticulously addressed all previous feedback, transforming the submission into a clean, professional, and fully compliant paper. The research itself is of the highest caliber,\
    \ presenting a simple yet highly impactful architectural modification that achieves a new state-of-the-art. The claims are rigorously supported by a well-designed 5-experiment study. The paper is a model of clarity, conciseness, and verifiability, representing a significant contribution to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future work, a promising direction could be to explore hybrid dilation strategies. Instead of using a uniform dilation factor across all layers, applying different rates at different depths of the CNN could offer a more nuanced way to expand the receptive field while potentially mitigating issues like gridding artifacts. This could lead to even more efficient and powerful spatial feature extractors.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy v3: Hybrid Dilated CNN (d=5) + Double-Step'\
    \ (ID: 154) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 62.3\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.678, 0.656, 0.616, 0.540]\nMean: 0.623, Std: 0.053\nBest trial: 1 (0.678)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n  dilation_rate: 5.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 928 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\n\
    Function sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  training_step: using default\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  BASE_SEED: 70709 (from submission)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune Sokoban RL Optimization (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 4 seeds, 50,000,000 steps per seed\nParallel trials: 4, Total trials: 4\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 1 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.80 | 0.58 | 3.36 | 5.22 | 8.66 | 8.27 | 9.13\nSolve\
    \ | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.188 | 0.350 | 0.600 | 0.556 | 0.643\n\nTimeout after 31201 updates (1807s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.678\n\nSeed 1 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.678\nTraining time: 1821s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 2 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M | 30.0M | 35.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | 0.30 | 3.04 | 3.76 | 5.53 | 5.92 | 7.60 | 8.86\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.167 | 0.222 | 0.357 | 0.385 | 0.500 | 0.632\n\nTimeout after 31101 updates (1808s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.656\n\nSeed 2 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.656\nTraining time: 1822s\n\nStarting Sokoban RL training - Seed 3 with timeout 30.0min\nPerforming initial network output shape validation...\nNetwork output shapes are correct.\nCompiling JAX functions...\n\nStep | 0.0M | 5.0M | 10.0M | 15.0M | 20.0M | 25.0M\
    \ | 30.0M | 35.0M\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nReturn | 0.00 | -0.53 | 0.13 | 1.10 | 3.18 | 7.16 | 8.79 | 8.54\nSolve | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.154 | 0.500 | 0.611 | 0.588\n\nTimeout after 30901 updates (1808s)\nFinal test solve rate: 0.616\n\nSeed 3 completed:\nSolve rate: 0.616\nTraining time: 1821s\nSUBMISSION_UUID: 2fad5819-7237-48e4-a68c-bcad3291d3ae\nTotal time: 1930s\n============================================================\nFull training completed!\n\n\nSTDERR:\n2025-09-13 16:49:56,263\tINFO worker.py:1747 -- Connecting to existing Ray cluster at address: 10.60.151.21:6379... timestamp_ns=1757753396263606453\n2025-09-13 16:49:56,278\tINFO worker.py:1927 -- Connected to Ray cluster. timestamp_ns=1757753396278746904\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request\
    \ that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software\
    \ and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\nGym has been unmaintained since 2022 and does not support NumPy 2.0 amongst other critical functionality.\nPlease upgrade to Gymnasium, the maintained drop-in replacement of Gym, or contact the authors of your software and request that they upgrade.\nUsers of this version of Gym should be able to simply replace 'import gym' with 'import gymnasium as gym' in the vast majority of cases.\nSee the migration guide at https://gymnasium.farama.org/introduction/migration_guide/ for additional information.\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 6` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed\
    \ for: 6. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 846 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated\
    \ ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
