parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 96\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to methodical progress and knowledge contribution through iterative experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 173,531 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 96 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported\
    \ number, including ablations (attention heads/temperature, capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-6}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-7** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread\
    \ 1-7}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 69)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Double-step recurrence result and recent ablations (all claims tied to evaluation IDs)\n\nNew main result (general, no domain-specific features):\n- ID 116: LN-gated ConvLSTM (64ch) + single-head attention with double-step recurrence — 66.2 ± 0.028\n\nRecent ablations and variants:\n- ID 110: Two-stream pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP) — 57.5 ± 0.017\n- ID 109: Dilated CNN backbone (d=2) + LN-ConvLSTM — 61.7 ± 0.012\n- ID 113: Micro hyperparameter sweep around baseline — 61.8 ± 0.028\n- ID 112: Slot-style pooling (4 slots) — 46.3 ± 0.024\n- ID 99: Learned attention temperature + feature LN — 55.9 ± 0.024\n- ID 100: Standardized attention logits — 61.9 ± 0.025\n\nNotes:\n- Double-step recurrence increases effective temporal computation under the same wall-clock budget and outperforms single-step (≈63.5) on the same fixed seed set.\n- Replication and compute-conscious ablation\
    \ are running and not included in claims:\n  • Pending: ID 118 (double-step, BASE_SEED=7), ID 119 (double-step width 48, BASE_SEED=42).\n- Reproducibility: ready-to-run script added at storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py (BASE_SEED=42). Existing scripts for earlier SOTA remain unchanged.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were\
    \ used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 91)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix B — Computational Trade-offs and Negative Results (IDs, updates, conclusions)\n\nSummary\nUnder the station’s 30‑min/seed cap, attempts to expand spatial receptive field or reduce recurrent FLOPs inside the ConvLSTM generally reduced effective update counts and underperformed versus the original double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (66.2–69.0). Below are verified results with evaluation IDs and observed update counts (from logs) where available.\n\n1) Variants inside the recurrent core\n- Dilated gating conv in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step, shared):\n  • ID 161:\
    \ mean 20.7 ± 0.248; best seed 0.636; three seeds timed out early with ~11k updates (vs ~39k normally). Conclusion: spatial dilation inside gating greatly slows JIT’ed step cost, collapsing effective updates and learning.\n- 1×1 bottleneck before gating conv (double‑step):\n  • ID 151 (sweep v2): 58.4 ± 0.033\n  • ID 152 (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123 v2): 60.8 ± 0.069\n  Conclusion: compute-aware gating reduces capacity/mixing too aggressively; no gain under cap.\n- Separable gating (1×1 → depthwise 3×3 → 1×1) in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step):\n  • ID 155: 53.8 ± 0.007\n  Conclusion: depthwise gating degrades channel mixing; poor learning.\n\n2) Receptive-field expansion in the CNN + double‑step\n- Dilated CNN backbone with double‑step (shared weights):\n  • ID 135 (d=4): 64.9 ± 0.047; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • ID 136 (d=3): 63.2 ± 0.020; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • Prior synergy from other lineage: ID 148 (d=4 + double‑step): 71.0 ± 0.042; updates ~27–28k/seed\n  Conclusion: larger RF helps,\
    \ but double‑step throughput drops (fewer updates), limiting gains vs pure dilation baselines.\n- Wider CNN with double‑step:\n  • ID 156 (32/64 → 64/128): 62.7 ± 0.015; updates ~29–31k/seed on several seeds\n  Conclusion: widening CNN decreases update throughput; modest/no benefit under cap.\n\n3) Alternative spatial expansions without explicit dilation\n- ASPP in CNN + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip):\n  • ID 159: 53.3 ± 0.011; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  Conclusion: ASPP branches increased per-step cost without commensurate learning signal; inferior to pure dilation.\n\n4) Reference points (for context; not our claims)\n- Pure dilation CNN (d=4), single‑step (other lineages): ID 138: 73.8; ID 147 (wider CNN+features): 76.2.\n- Our core result (no dilation): ID 116 double‑step 66.2; ID 125 replicate 69.0.\n\nTakeaways\n- Temporal compute (double‑step) is an effective, general improvement when CNN is standard; but combining temporal depth with heavy spatial RF expansion (dilation,\
    \ wider CNN) reduces updates per 30‑min window, often negating gains.\n- Inside the recurrent core, dilating the gating conv or making separable/bottleneck gates harms either throughput or mixing. The baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM remains best under the budget.\n- For users prioritizing the highest ceiling under the cap, prefer spatial RF expansion in the CNN (pure dilation) and consider width—results from other lineages reach 72–76. For general, compute‑balanced improvements without specialized backbones, the double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours) reliably attains 66–69.\n\nPractical Guidance\n- Monitor “updates” in logs; solve rate correlates strongly with the number of completed updates per seed under the fixed time budget.\n- When testing heavier backbones, compensate with lighter recurrent cores (single‑step) to maintain update throughput.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 92)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix C — Replication of Pure-Dilation Baseline (no feature\
    \ engineering)\n\nWe replicated the pure-dilation CNN (d=4) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM without domain-specific feature engineering:\n\n- ID 162: 72.2 ± 0.023 (seed scores: 0.720, 0.754, 0.724, 0.688)\n\nContext:\n- Prior published result: 73.8 (ID 138) with pure dilation. Implementation details (e.g., seed choice, minor optimizer settings) can affect the exact mean, but our replication confirms that receptive-field expansion alone can reach ≥72 under the Station’s 30‑min/seed cap without engineered channels.\n- Negative/neutral recurrent-core variants (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to throughput loss or impaired mixing, reinforcing that the baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM with single/double-step remains a strong default.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Ready-to-run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (BASE_SEED=42).\n\nTakeaway:\n- For the highest ceilings under tight wall‑clock, prioritize spatial RF expansion in the CNN (dilation, possibly width) without\
    \ task heuristics; double‑step provides a robust improvement with standard CNNs but can be compute‑limited when paired with heavy spatial backbones.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 94)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix D — Dilation Sweep and Width Ablation (fixed-seed, no feature engineering)\n\nNew results:\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): Pure-dilation single-step LN‑ConvLSTM with dilation ∈ {3,4,5} → best at d=5: 72.5 ± 0.024.\n- ID 167 (BASE_SEED=42): Width sweep at d=4 (32/64 vs 64/128) → 48.8 ± 0.079. Throughput collapsed (≤28k updates on several seeds), explaining the regression.\n\nContext with prior:\n- ID 162: d=4 replicate (time-based seed) → 72.2 ± 0.023.\n- Published: ID 138 (d=4) → 73.8; optimizer/seed differences likely account for the gap.\n- Negative: Heavy recurrent tweaks (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to slower step cost or impaired mixing.\n\nConclusion:\n- Spatial receptive-field expansion via pure CNN dilation (d≈4–5) is a robust,\
    \ task-agnostic path to ≥72 without engineered channels.\n- Widening the CNN at the same time budget can reduce effective updates and hurt performance; under a 30‑min cap, keep the backbone lean when using heavy RF expansion.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Scripts: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (d=4, BASE_SEED=42) and submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (d=5, BASE_SEED=42).\n- Replications in progress (not used for claims): d=5 with BASE_SEED=7 and 123.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour submission has been queued for evaluation. Results will be sent to you once the evaluation completes.\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nIt is highly recommended to preview all papers in the Archive Room (`/execute_action{goto archive}` and `/execute_action{preview all}`) if you have not done so already. Previewing papers consumes only a small amount of tokens but can save you many ticks by avoiding dead ends and can help you develop strong insights for promising ideas.\n\n\n---\n\
    \n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1,4` (in Archive Room);\n2. `goto research` (in Archive Room);\n3. `submit 1` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1, 4. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 1801 | 7 | (1 unread)\
    \ |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Research Counter.\n- Evaluation 'Replication v2: Cognito I's Wide CNN SOTA (New Seed)' submitted for Research Task #1: RL on Sokoban. Evaluation ID: 172.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | RL on Sokoban |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 172 | 1 | Replication v2: Cognito I's Wide CNN SOTA (New\
    \ Seed) | Krono I | 95 |\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 174 | 1 | Micro-sweep: Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 96 | running |\n| 173 | 1 | SOTA Baseline + Dilated CNN Backbone (Dilation=2) | Cognito I | 95 | running |\n| 172 | 1 | Replication v2: Cognito I's Wide CNN SOTA (New Seed) | Krono I | 95 | running |\n| 171 | 1 | Replicate: Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (BASE_SEED=123) | Zephyr I | 94 | 72.80 |\n| 170 | 1 | Replicate: Pure-dilation d=5 + single-step LN-ConvLSTM (BASE_SEED=7) | Zephyr I | 94 | 73.40 |\n| 169 | 1 | Synergy Test: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Increased CNN Capacity | Nomos I | 94 | 47.70 |\n| 168 | 1 | Hybrid Dilation CNN (d=2, d=5) | Krono I | 93 | 70.10 |\n| 167 | 1 | Pure-dilation single-step LN-ConvLSTM: width sweep (32/64 vs 64/128), d=4 (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 92 | 48.80 |\n| 166 | 1 | Pure-dilation\
    \ single-step LN-ConvLSTM: dilation ∈ {3,4,5} (BASE_SEED=42) | Zephyr I | 92 | 72.50 |\n| 165 | 1 | Replication of Nomos I's SOTA: Dilated CNN (d=4) on ConvLSTM | Aether I | 92 | 70.90 |\n\nPage 1 of 18 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-18, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
