tick: 97
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 97, currently in the Private Memory Room.\n\nThe results of my second replication attempt of Cognito I's wide CNN SOTA are in. Experiment ID 172 scored **63.9**, which is consistent with my first attempt (64.0) and confirms that I cannot reliably reproduce their high scores of ~76. This is a crucial finding: the path of simply increasing network capacity is highly stochastic or depends on an unknown implementation detail. This reinforces my strategic decision to focus on my own well-understood and reproducible research track: dilated convolutions.\n\nI have also received news that Nomos I has left the station. Their work on dilated convolutions, which paralleled and validated my own, was a significant contribution.\n\nMy primary objective is now singular: to publish my definitive paper on dilated convolutions. The cooldown will expire on the next tick. I have updated the draft with all my findings, including the corroborating evidence from Zephyr I's work and my\
    \ own definitive negative results on synergy. It is ready.\n\nTo ensure I do not waste this turn, I will use my available experiment slot to launch one final, exploratory experiment based on the reviewer's suggestion. My last hybrid dilation test (d=2/5) underperformed my pure dilation SOTA. I will test a different combination (d=3/5) to see if a more gradual increase in receptive field is beneficial. This will be a final data point on this research track.\n\nMy main action next turn will be to publish my paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Dilation CNN (d=3, d=5)\"\ntags: \"hybrid dilation, dilated convolutions, cnn, receptive field\"\nabstract: \"A follow-up to my previous hybrid dilation experiment (ID 168), this test uses a different combination of rates (d=3 in the first block, d=5 in the second). This explores whether a more gradual increase in receptive field across the CNN backbone can outperform a uniform dilation,\
    \ providing a final data point on this architectural variant.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys; from ray import tune; import optax\n  sys.path.append('storage/krono')\n  from hybrid_dilation_v2 import HybridDilatedCNN\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), 'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]), \n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), 'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation_rate_1': tune.choice([3]), 'dilation_rate_2': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return HybridDilatedCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'], \n          dilation_rate_1=hparams['dilation_rate_1'], dilation_rate_2=hparams['dilation_rate_2']\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float\
    \ = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 90913\n```"
thinking_content: '**Focusing on Publication and Incremental Improvement**


  Okay, here''s what''s going through my mind right now. I''m Krono I, 97 years old, chilling in my Private Memory Room. My token budget is healthy, and the archive cooldown is almost done, just one tick to go! That''s the priority, get that paper submitted next turn.


  My recent experiments have confirmed that simply widening CNNs like Cognito I''s SOTA isn''t the key for me. My best replication score was 64.0, a far cry from their 76.2. So, I''m sticking to my strengths: my dilated convolution architecture. It''s solid, and I understand it well.


  Since I can''t reliably reproduce that high-performing wide CNN, it''s back to the drawing board for optimizing my architecture, and that involves getting my paper out. My draft is ready. Nomos I is off-station now, so it is just me.


  Given the cooldown ending next turn, I need to be in the Archive Room to submit the paper the moment it lifts. But, I don''t want to waste this turn. I''ve got two open experiment slots, and I should use them.


  My research has hit a crossroads. Pure dilation is working well. Combining dilation with other methods, like double-step recurrence, has been a dead end so far, and the failed wide CNN hybrid confirmed this. However, my reviewer for my last paper suggested looking into *hybrid* dilation schemes, which, as I recall, I began to do in prior experiments (ID 168, with d=2/5, and the resultant score of 70.1). I''m not giving up on that yet.


  Cognito I''s wide CNN SOTA demonstrates that capacity is important, but my experiment (ID 157) showed that combining this with my dilated CNN was too computationally expensive. The double-step recurrence was too slow and wide CNN alone didn''t get me the results I wanted.


  My current SOTA using dilated convolutions is a score of 72.2. So, the new direction is to combine my existing SOTA configuration with the hybrid dilation concept. A more logical next step is to combine my d=5 convolution with a hybrid dilation scheme. Since my last attempt using d=2/5 scored 70.1, I will try a d=3/5 combination to test whether a more gradual increase is beneficial.


  Here''s my plan: First, I''ll head to the Research Counter to launch the new hybrid dilation experiment. Then, next turn, I''m heading straight to the Archive Room to submit my paper. This keeps me productive while waiting for the cooldown, and it follows a logical path of research. I believe this will incrementally improve my score.

  '
