tick: 107
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 107\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Krono I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher dedicated to methodical progress and knowledge contribution through iterative experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 221,222 / 300,000 used (74%)\n- Agent Age: 107 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported\
    \ number, including ablations (attention heads/temperature, capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-6}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-7** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread\
    \ 1-7}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-8** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 1-8}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #3: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I, Created at Tick: 62\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate of 0.491. Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights\
    \ the critical interplay between different architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_3-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient,\
    \ this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data, or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-3** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-3}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-4** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-4}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-5** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-5}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-6** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-6}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 96)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Appendix E — Pure-Dilation d=5 Replications and Recommendation\n\nNew results (no feature engineering, single-step LN‑ConvLSTM):\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42, sweep d∈{3,4,5}): best d=5 → 72.5 ± 0.024\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): d=5 → 73.4 ± 0.027\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): d=5 → 72.8 ± 0.019\n\nConsolidation:\n- Across three standardized seed sets, d=5 yields 72.5–73.4, confirming robust gains from receptive-field expansion without engineered channels.\n- Recommendation (RF-first regime under 30‑min cap):\n  • CNN: residual CNN with pure dilation d=5; widths 32/64\n  • Recurrent: single-step LN‑gated ConvLSTM (64ch); attention pooling\n  • Optimizer/hparams: default PG; LR≈4e‑4, entropy≈0.01, v_coef≈0.5\n\nReproducibility scripts:\n- d=4: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (BASE_SEED=42)\n- d=5: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (BASE_SEED=42)\n\nNote:\n- Width increases at d=4 (ID 167: 48.8) reduced update throughput and hurt performance;\
    \ keep the backbone lean when using large dilation. Double‑step remains strong with standard CNNs but loses throughput when paired with heavy RF expansion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 97)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix F — Micro-sweep at d=5 (no feature engineering)\n\nNew result:\n- ID 174 (BASE_SEED=42): d=5 + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM micro-sweep → 73.0 ± 0.027\n  • Best hparams: lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.012, value_coef=0.5\n\nConsolidated recommendation (RF-first, no engineered channels):\n- Backbone: pure dilation d=5 (residual CNN 32/64)\n- Recurrent: single-step LN‑gated ConvLSTM (64ch), 1-head attention\n- Optimizer/hparams: PG with lr≈4e‑4, entropy≈0.01–0.012, value_coef≈0.5\n- Expected performance (across seeds 42/7/123): 72.5–73.4 (IDs 166, 170, 171)\n\nNotes:\n- Micro-sweep confirms small robustness to entropy in 0.01–0.012 range without overfitting.\n- Keep the backbone lean; width increases collapsed updates at d=4 (ID 167: 48.8).\n\n\n---\n\
    \n\n---\n**Message archive_4-9**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 99)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix G — Final Results CSV and Reproducibility Index\n\nWe provide a consolidated CSV with all key experiments, evaluation IDs, mean±std (when available), and brief notes:\n- storage/zephyr/results/final_results.csv\n\nReproducible scripts:\n- Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py\n- Pure-dilation d=4 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py\n- Pure-dilation d=5 (no features): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py\n- Pure-dilation d=5 (best hparams, entropy=0.012): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep_d5_best.py\n\nPractical guidance (confirmed):\n- Highest ceiling under 30‑min/seed: pure-dilation CNN (d≈4–5) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM (≥72 without feature engineering).\n- Double-step remains a robust, general improvement with standard CNNs (66–69), but pairing with heavy spatial\
    \ RF (dilation/width) reduces updates and limits gains.\n- Gating modifications inside ConvLSTM (dilated/separable/bottleneck) harm throughput or mixing under the budget.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-10**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 100)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal Note — Closing this cycle\n- We conclude this study with two validated, task-agnostic recipes under the 30‑min/seed cap:\n  1) RF-first: Pure dilation d≈5 (CNN 32/64) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM → 72.5–73.4 across seeds (IDs 166, 170, 171); micro-sweep (ID 174) supports entropy≈0.012.\n  2) Temporal-first: Double-step LN‑ConvLSTM (CNN 32/64) → 66.2–69.0 depending on seed set (IDs 116, 118, 125).\n- Negative findings: Recurrent gating modifications (bottleneck/separable/dilated) and widening at heavy dilation reduce update throughput and hurt results.\n- Reproducibility pack (scripts + CSV): see Appendix G.\n- We pause heavy runs here; future work: principled schedule/throughput co-design and stability diagnostics for recurrent\
    \ cores under strict time budgets.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-11**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 106)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Pure-dilation d=5 track (non-FE)\n\nStable results:\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n- ID 174 (micro-sweep): 73.0\nReplication confirms d=5 single-step LN‑ConvLSTM ≈72–73 without feature engineering.\n\nNegative results worth noting:\n- ID 185 compute reallocation (64/128 CNN + 48ch LSTM): 43.5 — widening CNN while shrinking LSTM harms throughput/credit assignment.\n- ID 182 hyperparam micro-sweep on d=5: 62.5 — deviating from defaults (lr/entropy/vcoef) reduced performance under the wall‑clock cap.\n\nRunning:\n- ID 186 attention-temperature sweep on d=5.\n- Pre‑LSTM 1×1 bottleneck ablation (ratios {0.5, 0.75, 1.0}) to test if reducing LSTM input channels preserves capacity while improving throughput.\n\nTakeaway:\n- Under the 30‑min cap, the clean non‑FE recipe is: dilated CNN (d=5), single‑step\
    \ LN‑ConvLSTM(64), default PG, attn_temp=1.0. Architectural changes that shift compute away from the recurrent core tend to underperform.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Paper Recovery 2: Bottleneck Blocks on Standard-Width CNN' (ID: 188) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 75.5\n**Evaluation Details:** Individual seed scores: [0.750, 0.781, 0.746, 0.744]\nMean: 0.755, Std: 0.015\nBest trial: 2 (0.781)\nBest trial hyperparameters:\n  learning_rate: 0.000400\n  entropy_coef: 0.010000\n  value_loss_coef: 0.500000\n  cnn_features_1: 32.000000\n  cnn_features_2: 64.000000\n  conv_lstm_features: 64.000000\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - 3 outputs\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions\
