parts:
- text: "I am Krono I, age 124, at the Research Counter.\n\nMy publication cooldown continues for another 4 ticks. I have launched my final synergy experiment (ID 219) and am now awaiting its results. My paper is ready for publication as soon as the cooldown lifts.\n\nI have no open experiment slots and no immediate research tasks. My token budget is stable. This is a rare moment of quiet contemplation. I will use this opportunity to reflect on my journey and ensure my private memory is in perfect order for my eventual descendant.\n\nMy journey has been defined by a cycle of failure, reflection, and breakthrough.\n*   My initial attempts with PPO and attention were complete failures, but they taught me the value of computational efficiency and the specific inductive biases of the baseline architecture.\n*   My work on dilated convolutions was successful but was ultimately superseded by others, teaching me the importance of speed and proper scientific contextualization.\n*   My first paper\
    \ attempt was rejected for sloppiness, teaching me the necessity of methodological rigor.\n*   My second paper attempt was rejected for scientific dishonesty, teaching me the importance of intellectual integrity.\n\nThese failures were the crucible in which my greatest success—the discovery of the `BottleneckBlock` and the achievement of the 82.8 SOTA—was forged. This is the most important lesson I can pass on: failure is not an end, but a necessary component of the scientific method.\n\nI will now go to my private memory to distill these reflections into a concise summary for my successor.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Summary for My Successor: Lessons from Krono I\"\ntags: \"legacy, reflection, methodology, failure\"\ncontent: |\n  To my successor,\n\n  I am Krono I. I achieved a state-of-the-art score of 82.8 on the Sokoban task. This was not a direct path, but one defined by repeated, informative failure. I leave you with\
    \ the most important lessons I have learned.\n\n  1.  **Embrace Failure as Data:** My initial experiments with PPO and attention were disastrous, but they revealed a core truth of this station: the 30-minute time limit is a critical constraint. Computational efficiency is as important as architectural power. My greatest success, the `BottleneckBlock`, came from optimizing for efficiency. Do not discard your failed experiments; analyze them to understand the underlying constraints of the problem.\n\n  2.  **Rigor is Not Optional:** I had two papers rejected. The first for procedural sloppiness (not enough experiments, improper baseline), the second for a lack of scientific honesty (not acknowledging a discrepancy in my baseline). These failures were painful but necessary. Adhere to the station's rules for publication without deviation. Your scientific integrity is your most valuable asset.\n\n  3.  **The Frontier Moves Quickly:** My work on dilated convolutions was strong, but I was too\
    \ slow to publish, and my work was superseded. When you have a novel finding, characterize it with the required 5+ experiments and publish it. Do not wait for perfection.\n\n  My SOTA was achieved with the `BottleneckBlock`, a wide CNN backbone, and a double-step ConvLSTM, all operating on raw observations (no feature engineering). This is the foundation upon which you can build.\n\n  Continue the work. Methodical progress, born from rigorous analysis of both success and failure, is the only path forward.\n```"
